%% A reading map of Roger Penrose, The Road to Reality: one top-level node per chapter, holding the
%% mathematical and physical concepts that chapter introduces first and the questions it raises. `needs`
%% edges point from a concept to what it builds on; labeled `relates` edges tie questions to the concepts
%% they involve. Links go to the chapter pages of the annotated online edition (Chinese translation).
%% The Chinese version is road-to-reality.zh.rhumb, with the same IDs and structure.

- [ ] 1. The roots of science {kind: chapter} ^ch01
  - Penrose argues that mathematical proof was the first breakthrough of science and introduces his three worlds (Platonic-mathematical, physical, mental) and the three mysteries linking them. [Chapter 1](r2r:chapter_01)
  - [ ] Platonic solids {kind: math} ^platonic-solids
    - The five regular polyhedra, which the ancient Greeks superstitiously matched with the elements and the heavens. [§1.1](r2r:chapter_01#11-探寻世界的成因)
  - [ ] Mathematical proof {kind: math} ^mathematical-proof
    - A watertight chain of logical deduction from axioms, credited to Thales and Pythagoras as the first cornerstone of science. [§1.2](r2r:chapter_01#12-数学真理)
  - [ ] Axioms and theorems {kind: math} ^axioms
    - Self-evident starting propositions from which proof establishes theorems; geometric 'axioms' turn out to be questionable. [§1.2](r2r:chapter_01#12-数学真理)
  - [ ] Euclidean geometry {kind: math} ^euclidean-geometry
    - The axiomatic geometry of rigid bodies in three-dimensional space, an excellent but not exact description of physical space. [§1.2](r2r:chapter_01#12-数学真理)
  - [ ] Platonic world of mathematical forms {kind: math} ^platonic-world
    - Plato's realm of idealized mathematical objects, of which physical squares, circles and cubes are only approximations. [§1.2](r2r:chapter_01#12-数学真理)
  - [ ] Objectivity of mathematical truth {kind: math} ^mathematical-objectivity
    - Penrose's reading of Platonic existence: mathematical truths hold independently of any person or culture, illustrated by Fermat and Mandelbrot. [§1.3](r2r:chapter_01#13-柏拉图的数学世界真实吗)
  - [ ] Fermat's Last Theorem {kind: math} ^fermat-last-theorem
    - No positive integers satisfy x^n + y^n = z^n for n > 2; proved by Wiles, used as a test case for objectivity. [§1.3](r2r:chapter_01#13-柏拉图的数学世界真实吗)
  - [ ] Three worlds and three mysteries {kind: math} ^three-worlds
    - Penrose's picture of Platonic-mathematical, physical and mental worlds, each linked mysteriously to a small part of the previous one. [§1.4](r2r:chapter_01#14-三个世界与三重奥秘)
  - [ ] Is Plato's mathematical world real? {kind: question} ^q-platonic-world-real
    - Whether mathematical objects exist objectively, independent of minds, or are cultural products of mathematicians' agreement. [§1.3](r2r:chapter_01#13-柏拉图的数学世界真实吗)
  - [ ] Why does mathematics govern the physical world so precisely? {kind: question} ^q-why-math-describes-physics
    - The first and central mystery of the book: the extraordinary precision and beauty with which mathematics describes physical behaviour. [§1.4](r2r:chapter_01#14-三个世界与三重奥秘)
  - [ ] How does mind arise from physical structure? {kind: question} ^q-mind-from-matter
    - The second mystery: how suitably organized matter such as a brain comes to have conscious mental activity. [§1.4](r2r:chapter_01#14-三个世界与三重奥秘)
  - [ ] How can each world encompass the one it projects from? {kind: question} ^q-three-worlds-paradox
    - The deliberate paradox in the three-worlds figure, hinting that the worlds may be aspects of one deeper reality. [§1.5](r2r:chapter_01#15-善真美)

- [ ] 2. An ancient theorem and a modern question {kind: chapter} ^ch02
  - Proofs of the Pythagorean theorem expose its dependence on Euclid's parallel postulate, whose failure yields hyperbolic geometry, shown in several Euclidean models and tied to the geometry of the universe. [Chapter 2](r2r:chapter_02)
  - [ ] Pythagorean theorem {kind: math} ^pythagorean-theorem
    - The squared hypotenuse equals the sum of squares of the other sides; proved here by a tiling of two square sizes. [§2.1](r2r:chapter_02#21-毕达哥拉斯定理)
  - [ ] Euclid's postulates {kind: math} ^euclid-postulates
    - Euclid's five assumptions of plane geometry; the first four express straight lines, circles, unboundedness, homogeneity and isotropy. [§2.2](r2r:chapter_02#22-欧几里得公设)
  - [ ] Parallel postulate {kind: math} ^parallel-postulate
    - Euclid's fifth postulate, equivalent to Playfair's axiom of unique parallels; needed even to prove that squares exist. [§2.2](r2r:chapter_02#22-欧几里得公设)
  - [ ] Angle sum of a triangle {kind: math} ^triangle-angle-sum
    - In Euclidean geometry a triangle's angles sum to π, a fact whose standard proof uses the parallel postulate. [§2.3](r2r:chapter_02#23-毕达哥拉斯定理的相似面积证明)
  - [ ] Similar figures and area scaling {kind: math} ^similar-triangles
    - Similar figures have areas proportional to squared linear size, giving a second proof of Pythagoras via a split right triangle. [§2.3](r2r:chapter_02#23-毕达哥拉斯定理的相似面积证明)
  - [ ] Hyperbolic geometry {kind: math} ^hyperbolic-geometry
    - Consistent non-Euclidean geometry where the parallel postulate fails, triangles' angles sum to less than π and Pythagoras fails. [§2.4](r2r:chapter_02#24-双曲几何共形图像)
  - [ ] Conformal (Poincaré disc) model {kind: math} ^poincare-disc
    - Hyperbolic plane drawn inside a circle with lines as orthogonal circular arcs and true angles, as in Escher's Circle Limit I. [§2.4](r2r:chapter_02#24-双曲几何共形图像)
  - [ ] Lambert's area formula {kind: math} ^lambert-area-formula
    - A hyperbolic triangle's area is fixed by its angles, π − (α+β+γ) = CΔ, the analogue of Harriot's spherical formula. [§2.4](r2r:chapter_02#24-双曲几何共形图像)
  - [ ] Hyperbolic distance and pseudo-radius {kind: math} ^hyperbolic-distance
    - Distance in the disc model as the log of a cross-ratio, scaled by the pseudo-radius C^(-1/2). [§2.4](r2r:chapter_02#24-双曲几何共形图像)
  - [ ] Projective (Beltrami-Klein) model {kind: math} ^beltrami-klein-model
    - Hyperbolic plane in a disc with straight chords as lines but distorted angles; linked to the conformal model via Beltrami's hemisphere. [§2.5](r2r:chapter_02#25-双曲几何的其他表示)
  - [ ] Stereographic projection {kind: math} ^stereographic-projection
    - Projection of a sphere from its pole onto a plane, conformal and circle-preserving; maps the hemisphere model to the conformal one. [§2.5](r2r:chapter_02#25-双曲几何的其他表示)
  - [ ] Proof by contradiction {kind: math} ^proof-by-contradiction
    - Assume a statement false and derive a contradiction; Saccheri's attempt this way on the fifth postulate uncovered hyperbolic geometry instead. [§2.6](r2r:chapter_02#26-双曲几何的历史渊源)
  - [ ] Pseudosphere {kind: math} ^pseudosphere
    - Surface of revolution of a tractrix with constant negative intrinsic curvature, giving Beltrami's local model of hyperbolic geometry. [§2.6](r2r:chapter_02#26-双曲几何的历史渊源)
  - [ ] Elliptic geometry {kind: math} ^elliptic-geometry
    - Finite, positively curved homogeneous geometry of figures on a sphere; one of three isotropic geometries with Euclidean and hyperbolic. [§2.7](r2r:chapter_02#27-与物理空间的关系)
  - [ ] How do we know squares exist? {kind: question} ^q-do-squares-exist
    - Proving that a figure with equal sides and four right angles exists turns out to depend on the parallel postulate. [§2.1](r2r:chapter_02#21-毕达哥拉斯定理)
  - [ ] Can the parallel postulate be proved from the others? {kind: question} ^q-parallel-postulate-provable
    - A centuries-long quest ended by showing hyperbolic geometry is consistent, so the fifth postulate is independent. [§2.6](r2r:chapter_02#26-双曲几何的历史渊源)
  - [ ] Is the universe's spatial geometry Euclidean, hyperbolic or elliptic? {kind: question} ^q-geometry-of-universe
    - An open observational question on cosmological scales; Penrose admits a preference for the hyperbolic case. [§2.7](r2r:chapter_02#27-与物理空间的关系)

- [ ] 3. Kinds of number in the physical world {kind: chapter} ^ch03
  - Starting from the irrationality of √2, the chapter builds the real numbers and asks how naturals, integers and reals connect to physics, from charge quantization to the continuum. [Chapter 3](r2r:chapter_03)
  - [ ] Rational numbers {kind: math} ^rational-numbers
    - Ratios a/b of integers, which the Pythagoreans hoped would suffice to measure all geometrical lengths. [§3.1](r2r:chapter_03#31-毕达哥拉斯灾难)
  - [ ] Irrationality of √2 {kind: math} ^irrationality-of-sqrt2
    - Proof by infinite descent that no fraction squares to 2, although Pythagoras gives the unit square diagonal √2. [§3.1](r2r:chapter_03#31-毕达哥拉斯灾难)
  - [ ] Real numbers {kind: math} ^real-numbers
    - Numbers given by infinite decimal or continued-fraction expansions, needed for Euclidean lengths and the framework of all successful physics. [§3.2](r2r:chapter_03#32-实数系)
  - [ ] Continued fractions {kind: math} ^continued-fractions
    - Nested expressions a+1/(b+1/(c+…)) that the Greeks used for ratios; finite exactly for rationals. [§3.2](r2r:chapter_03#32-实数系)
  - [ ] Quadratic irrationals {kind: math} ^quadratic-irrationals
    - Numbers a+√b, constructible by ruler and compass; Lagrange showed they are exactly those with eventually periodic continued fractions. [§3.2](r2r:chapter_03#32-实数系)
  - [ ] Eudoxan theory of ratios {kind: math} ^eudoxus-ratios
    - Eudoxus' criterion for comparing length ratios via integer multiples, anticipating the modern real number concept. [§3.2](r2r:chapter_03#32-实数系)
  - [ ] Dedekind cuts {kind: math} ^dedekind-cuts
    - Dedekind's construction of each real number as a partition of the rationals, modernizing Eudoxus. [§3.2](r2r:chapter_03#32-实数系)
  - [ ] Limiting processes {kind: math} ^limits
    - Reals give meaning to infinite sums and sequences, e.g. 1−1/3+1/5−… = π/4, underpinning area and calculus. [§3.2](r2r:chapter_03#32-实数系)
  - [ ] Natural numbers {kind: math} ^natural-numbers
    - The counting numbers 0, 1, 2, 3, … with addition and multiplication, whose dependence on physical 'things' is questioned. [§3.4](r2r:chapter_03#34-自然数需要物理世界吗)
  - [ ] Von Neumann ordinals {kind: math} ^von-neumann-ordinals
    - Natural numbers built from the empty set alone: ∅, {∅}, {∅,{∅}}, …, independent of the physical world. [§3.4](r2r:chapter_03#34-自然数需要物理世界吗)
  - [ ] Integers {kind: math} ^integers
    - Natural numbers and their negatives; their direct physical meaning only became clear in the twentieth century. [§3.5](r2r:chapter_03#35-物理世界里的离散数)
  - [ ] Quantization of electric charge {kind: physics} ^electric-charge-quantization
    - Every isolated body's charge is an integer multiple of the proton charge (a third of it for quarks). [§3.5](r2r:chapter_03#35-物理世界里的离散数)
  - [ ] Additive quantum numbers {kind: physics} ^additive-quantum-numbers
    - Particle labels like charge that add over constituents; all known ones take integer values. [§3.5](r2r:chapter_03#35-物理世界里的离散数)
  - [ ] Antiparticles {kind: physics} ^antiparticles
    - Dirac's partners with negated additive quantum numbers but the same positive mass, so an antiproton is not 'minus a proton'. [§3.5](r2r:chapter_03#35-物理世界里的离散数)
  - [ ] The Pythagorean catastrophe {kind: question} ^q-pythagorean-catastrophe
    - If no rational number measures a square's diagonal, geometry cannot rest on fractions alone. [§3.1](r2r:chapter_03#31-毕达哥拉斯灾难)
  - [ ] Is physical reality continuous or discrete? {kind: question} ^q-continuum-or-discrete
    - Real numbers work across about 10^43 scales, yet Schrödinger, Einstein and Penrose's spin networks suggest discreteness may be fundamental. [§3.3](r2r:chapter_03#33-物理世界里的实数)
  - [ ] Do natural numbers need the physical world? {kind: question} ^q-natural-numbers-physical
    - Whether counting numbers depend on persistent physical objects, or can be conjured from pure set theory. [§3.4](r2r:chapter_03#34-自然数需要物理世界吗)

- [ ] 4. Magical complex numbers {kind: chapter} ^ch04
  - Adjoining i = √−1 gives a number system where every polynomial has roots, the convergence of power series becomes clear in the Wessel plane, and the Mandelbrot set lives. [Chapter 4](r2r:chapter_04)
  - [ ] Complex numbers {kind: math} ^complex-numbers
    - Numbers a+ib with i² = −1, closed under all arithmetic operations; introduced by Cardano and Bombelli. [Chapter 4](r2r:chapter_04)
  - [ ] Fundamental theorem of algebra {kind: math} ^fundamental-theorem-of-algebra
    - Every complex polynomial equation has a complex solution, so it factorizes into linear factors. [§4.2](r2r:chapter_04#42-用复数解方程)
  - [ ] Cardano's formula for the cubic {kind: math} ^cubic-formula
    - Solution of x³ = 3px + 2q; in the irreducible case three real roots are reachable only through complex numbers. [§4.2](r2r:chapter_04#42-用复数解方程)
  - [ ] Power series {kind: math} ^power-series
    - Infinite sums a0 + a1x + a2x² + …, which converge to a finite value for some x and diverge for others. [§4.3](r2r:chapter_04#43-幂级数的收敛)
  - [ ] Divergent series {kind: math} ^divergent-series
    - Series like Euler's 1+4+16+… = −1/3, nonsense literally yet meaningful with care, as in quantum field theory. [§4.3](r2r:chapter_04#43-幂级数的收敛)
  - [ ] Complex plane (Wessel plane) {kind: math} ^complex-plane
    - Representation of z = x+iy as the point (x, y), with real and imaginary axes extending the real line. [§4.4](r2r:chapter_04#44-韦塞尔复平面)
  - [ ] Singularities and poles {kind: math} ^poles
    - Points where a function becomes infinite; (1+z²)⁻¹ has hidden poles at ±i invisible on the real line. [§4.4](r2r:chapter_04#44-韦塞尔复平面)
  - [ ] Circle of convergence {kind: math} ^circle-of-convergence
    - A power series converges inside and diverges outside a circle about the origin reaching the nearest singularity. [§4.4](r2r:chapter_04#44-韦塞尔复平面)
  - [ ] Mandelbrot set {kind: math} ^mandelbrot-set
    - The points c for which iterating z ↦ z² + c from 0 stays bounded; simple rule, endlessly intricate boundary. [§4.5](r2r:chapter_04#45-如何构造曼德布罗特集)
  - [ ] Why does nature use complex numbers? {kind: question} ^q-why-nature-uses-complex-numbers
    - Complex numbers, long seen as an invention, turn out to govern quantum physics; Penrose calls this magical. [Chapter 4](r2r:chapter_04)
  - [ ] Why does the series for 1/(1+x²) diverge beyond |x| = 1? {kind: question} ^q-hidden-divergence
    - The function is smooth on the whole real line, yet its series fails beyond ±1; complex poles at ±i explain this. [§4.3](r2r:chapter_04#43-幂级数的收敛)
  - [ ] What can a divergent series' 'sum' mean? {kind: question} ^q-meaning-of-divergent-series
    - Whether answers like Euler's −1/3 carry real meaning, an issue that recurs in quantum field theory. [§4.3](r2r:chapter_04#43-幂级数的收敛)

- [ ] 5. Geometry of logarithms, powers, and roots {kind: chapter} ^ch05
  - Complex arithmetic is shown geometrically, logarithms and complex powers are defined with their multivaluedness, and roots of unity lead to multiplicative quantum numbers in particle physics. [Chapter 5](r2r:chapter_05)
  - [ ] Geometry of complex addition and multiplication {kind: math} ^complex-multiplication-geometry
    - Adding w translates the plane; multiplying by w rotates and scales it, so multiplying by i is a right-angle turn. [§5.1](r2r:chapter_05#51-复代数几何)
  - [ ] Modulus and argument (polar form) {kind: math} ^polar-coordinates
    - Writing z by its distance r = |z| from 0 and angle θ, defined only up to multiples of 2π. [§5.1](r2r:chapter_05#51-复代数几何)
  - [ ] Logarithm {kind: math} ^logarithm
    - Inverse of exponentiation that turns multiplication into addition, as on a slide rule. [§5.2](r2r:chapter_05#52-复对数概念)
  - [ ] Exponential function and e {kind: math} ^exponential-function
    - e^z defined by the series Σ zⁿ/n!, convergent everywhere, with e^(a+b) = e^a e^b. [§5.3](r2r:chapter_05#53-多值性自然对数)
  - [ ] Complex (natural) logarithm {kind: math} ^complex-logarithm
    - log w = log r + iθ; its imaginary part is the argument, so it is defined only up to multiples of 2πi. [§5.3](r2r:chapter_05#53-多值性自然对数)
  - [ ] Euler's identity {kind: math} ^euler-identity
    - e^(πi) + 1 = 0 links 0, 1, i, π and e; with e^(2πi) = 1 it underlies the logarithm's ambiguity. [§5.3](r2r:chapter_05#53-多值性自然对数)
  - [ ] Cotes-Euler formula {kind: math} ^euler-formula
    - e^(iθ) = cos θ + i sin θ, which packs the trigonometric addition and multiple-angle formulas into the exponential. [§5.3](r2r:chapter_05#53-多值性自然对数)
  - [ ] Complex powers {kind: math} ^complex-powers
    - w^z = e^(z log w), well defined only once a branch of log w is specified; i^i is real. [§5.4](r2r:chapter_05#54-复数幂)
  - [ ] Equiangular (logarithmic) spiral {kind: math} ^equiangular-spiral
    - Curve meeting every ray from the pole at the same angle; the many values of w^z lie at intersections of two such spirals. [§5.4](r2r:chapter_05#54-复数幂)
  - [ ] Roots of unity {kind: math} ^roots-of-unity
    - The n values e^(2πik/n) of 1^(1/n), the vertices of a regular n-gon on the unit circle. [§5.4](r2r:chapter_05#54-复数幂)
  - [ ] Cyclic group Z_n {kind: math} ^cyclic-group
    - The nth roots of unity form a finite multiplicative group, e.g. 1, ω, ω² acting as rotations of a triangle. [§5.4](r2r:chapter_05#54-复数幂)
  - [ ] Multiplicative quantum numbers {kind: physics} ^multiplicative-quantum-numbers
    - Particle labels that multiply over constituents, taking nth-root-of-unity values; most are only approximately valid. [Chapter 5](r2r:chapter_05)
  - [ ] Parity {kind: physics} ^parity
    - An approximate n = 2 multiplicative quantum number ε = ±1 under mirror reflection, not conserved by weak interactions. [Chapter 5](r2r:chapter_05)
  - [ ] Quarkiness {kind: physics} ^quarkiness
    - Penrose's n = 3 multiplicative quantum number e^(−2qπi) with Z_3 values; free particles must have quarkiness 1. [Chapter 5](r2r:chapter_05)
  - [ ] Why plane rotations? What about three dimensions? {kind: question} ^q-why-only-plane-rotations
    - Complex multiplication captures rotations of a plane; Penrose asks what plays this role in 3D, revisited with quaternions later. [§5.1](r2r:chapter_05#51-复代数几何)
  - [ ] The paradox e = e\^(1−4π²) {kind: question} ^q-complex-power-paradox
    - Exercise 5.10's apparent contradiction from treating powers as single-valued, resolved by fixing a branch of the logarithm. [§5.4](r2r:chapter_05#54-复数幂)
  - [ ] What is i\^i? {kind: question} ^q-i-to-the-i
    - Once thought the height of the imaginary, i^i is in fact real, e^(−π/2), with infinitely many real values. [§5.4](r2r:chapter_05#54-复数幂)

- [ ] 6. Real-number calculus {kind: chapter} ^ch06
  - Introduces differentiation, smoothness classes, power-series analyticity and integration for real functions, contrasting the modern mapping notion of function with Euler's. [Chapter 6](r2r:chapter_06)
  - [ ] Function as a mapping {kind: math} ^function-as-mapping
    - Modern view of a function as a map from a domain to a target, a look-up table needing no explicit formula. [§6.1](r2r:chapter_06#61-如何构造实函数)
  - [ ] Heaviside step function {kind: math} ^heaviside-step-function
    - theta(x), zero for negative x and one for positive x; a perfectly good modern function that Euler would reject. [§6.1](r2r:chapter_06#61-如何构造实函数)
  - [ ] Continuity {kind: math} ^continuity
    - A function is continuous where its graph has no jump; theta(x) fails at the origin, |x| does not. [§6.2](r2r:chapter_06#62-函数的斜率)
  - [ ] Derivative {kind: math} ^derivative
    - The slope dy/dx of a graph at a point, defined by a limit; |x| is not differentiable at its corner. [§6.2](r2r:chapter_06#62-函数的斜率)
  - [ ] Higher derivatives {kind: math} ^higher-derivatives
    - Repeated differentiation; zeros of f' give maxima and minima, zeros of f'' give inflection points related to curvature. [§6.3](r2r:chapter_06#63-高阶导数cinfty-光滑函数)
  - [ ] C\^n and C\^infinity smoothness {kind: math} ^smooth-functions
    - C^n means n continuous derivatives; C^infinity means all orders, yet smooth glued functions like h(x) still exist. [§6.3](r2r:chapter_06#63-高阶导数cinfty-光滑函数)
  - [ ] Taylor and Maclaurin series {kind: math} ^taylor-series
    - Power-series coefficients a_n = f^(n)(p)/n! fixed by a function's derivatives at the expansion point. [§6.4](r2r:chapter_06#64-欧拉的-函数概念)
  - [ ] Analytic (C\^omega) functions {kind: math} ^analytic-functions
    - Functions locally equal to their power series everywhere; stricter than C^infinity, as exp(-1/x) glued to zero shows. [§6.4](r2r:chapter_06#64-欧拉的-函数概念)
  - [ ] Leibniz rule and differentiation rules {kind: math} ^leibniz-rule
    - Product rule d(fg) = f dg + g df, with sum, power, chain and quotient rules making differentiation mechanical. [§6.5](r2r:chapter_06#65-微分法则)
  - [ ] Indefinite and definite integrals {kind: math} ^integration
    - Integration inverts differentiation up to a constant; the definite integral gives signed area between two limits. [§6.6](r2r:chapter_06#66-积分)
  - [ ] Fundamental theorem of calculus {kind: math} ^fundamental-theorem-of-calculus
    - Area under the curve f equals the difference g(b) - g(a) of any function g whose derivative is f. [§6.6](r2r:chapter_06#66-积分)
  - [ ] Dirac delta function {kind: math} ^dirac-delta-function
    - Derivative of the step function, infinite at the origin; a C^-2 'function' vital to quantum mechanics. [§6.6](r2r:chapter_06#66-积分)
  - [ ] What should count as a genuine function? {kind: question} ^q-what-is-a-function
    - Can Euler's intuition of a single unglued function be captured by smoothness, power series, or complex differentiability? [§6.4](r2r:chapter_06#64-欧拉的-函数概念)
  - [ ] Why is differentiation easy and integration hard (and vice versa)? {kind: question} ^q-differentiation-vs-integration
    - Explicit formulas differentiate mechanically but integrals need new functions; numerically and for existence, integration smooths while differentiation degrades. [§6.6](r2r:chapter_06#66-积分)

- [ ] 7. Complex-number calculus {kind: chapter} ^ch07
  - Shows that complex differentiability, via contour integration and Cauchy's formula, forces analyticity, and develops analytic continuation and its path dependence. [Chapter 7](r2r:chapter_07)
  - [ ] Holomorphic functions {kind: math} ^holomorphic-functions
    - Complex-smooth functions of a complex variable; the book's central notion, which turns out to be complex-analytic automatically. [§7.1](r2r:chapter_07#71-复光滑全纯函数)
  - [ ] Contour integration {kind: math} ^contour-integration
    - Integrals along paths in the complex plane, unchanged under deformation in the domain; around the origin, the integral of dz/z is 2 pi i. [§7.2](r2r:chapter_07#72-周线积分)
  - [ ] Homologous and homotopic deformations {kind: math} ^homology-homotopy
    - Path deformations allowing (homology) or forbidding (homotopy) cancellation of oppositely directed pieces; homotopic paths are always homologous. [§7.2](r2r:chapter_07#72-周线积分)
  - [ ] Cauchy integral formula {kind: math} ^cauchy-integral-formula
    - A holomorphic function's value and nth derivative at p are given by contour integrals of f/(z-p)^(n+1). [§7.3](r2r:chapter_07#73-复光滑幂级数)
  - [ ] Complex smoothness implies analyticity {kind: math} ^complex-smooth-implies-analytic
    - Using Cauchy's formula, a once complex-differentiable function has convergent Taylor series, hence derivatives of all orders. [§7.3](r2r:chapter_07#73-复光滑幂级数)
  - [ ] Residue theorem {kind: math} ^residue-theorem
    - A closed contour integral equals 2 pi i times the sum of enclosed residues; used for definite integrals and series sums. [§7.3](r2r:chapter_07#73-复光滑幂级数)
  - [ ] Open regions {kind: math} ^open-regions
    - A set where every point has a small disc around it still inside; the open unit disc qualifies, the closed one does not. [§7.4](r2r:chapter_07#74-解析延拓)
  - [ ] Analytic continuation {kind: math} ^analytic-continuation
    - Extending a holomorphic function by overlapping power series along a path; unique per path, rigid, but path-dependent, as for log z. [§7.4](r2r:chapter_07#74-解析延拓)
  - [ ] Riemann zeta function {kind: math} ^riemann-zeta-function
    - Dirichlet series sum of n^-z, continued analytically to the whole plane except z = 1; central to prime-number theory. [§7.4](r2r:chapter_07#74-解析延拓)
  - [ ] Riemann hypothesis {kind: question} ^q-riemann-hypothesis
    - Do all non-trivial zeros of the zeta function lie on Re(z) = 1/2? Perhaps the most important unsolved mathematical problem. [§7.4](r2r:chapter_07#74-解析延拓)
  - [ ] Why does complex smoothness give so much? {kind: question} ^q-magic-of-complex-smoothness
    - The proof that one complex derivative implies analyticity routes through contour integrals and the multivalued logarithm, absent from statement and conclusion. [§7.3](r2r:chapter_07#73-复光滑幂级数)

- [ ] 8. Riemann surfaces and complex mappings {kind: chapter} ^ch08
  - Treats multivalued holomorphic functions as single-valued on Riemann surfaces, links holomorphy to conformal maps, and introduces the Riemann sphere, genus, moduli and the Riemann mapping theorem. [Chapter 8](r2r:chapter_08)
  - [ ] Riemann surfaces {kind: math} ^riemann-surfaces
    - Multi-sheeted domains, glued from complex-plane patches, on which multivalued functions like log z become single-valued; the first example of a manifold. [§8.1](r2r:chapter_08#81-黎曼曲面概念)
  - [ ] Branch points {kind: math} ^branch-points
    - Points around which a function's sheets permute; order n for z^(m/n), infinite order for log z. [§8.1](r2r:chapter_08#81-黎曼曲面概念)
  - [ ] Conformal mappings {kind: math} ^conformal-mappings
    - Maps preserving angles and infinitesimal shapes; holomorphic maps are exactly the conformal, orientation-preserving ones. [§8.2](r2r:chapter_08#82-共形映射)
  - [ ] Mobius (bilinear) transformations {kind: math} ^mobius-transformations
    - Maps w = (az+b)/(cz+d), ad not equal to bc, combining Euclidean motions, scalings and inversion; they send circles to circles. [§8.2](r2r:chapter_08#82-共形映射)
  - [ ] Riemann sphere {kind: math} ^riemann-sphere
    - Complex plane plus infinity, built from z and w = 1/z patches; stereographically a sphere, the simplest compact Riemann surface. [§8.3](r2r:chapter_08#83-黎曼球面)
  - [ ] Genus of a compact Riemann surface {kind: math} ^genus
    - Number of handles that classifies compact orientable surfaces topologically: sphere 0, torus or teacup 1, pretzel 3. [§8.4](r2r:chapter_08#84-紧黎曼曲面的亏格)
  - [ ] Moduli of Riemann surfaces {kind: math} ^moduli
    - Complex parameters distinguishing conformally inequivalent surfaces of one genus: one for tori, 3g-3 for g >= 2, with m - s = 3g - 3. [§8.4](r2r:chapter_08#84-紧黎曼曲面的亏格)
  - [ ] Riemann mapping theorem {kind: math} ^riemann-mapping-theorem
    - Any region bounded by a simple closed loop maps holomorphically onto the unit disc, uniquely once three boundary points are fixed. [§8.5](r2r:chapter_08#85-黎曼映射定理)
  - [ ] Zhukovsky aerofoil transformation {kind: physics} ^zhukovsky-transformation
    - w = (z + 1/z)/2 maps a circle's exterior to an aerofoil, making idealized airflow past a wing calculable. [§8.5](r2r:chapter_08#85-黎曼映射定理)
  - [ ] How should multivalued functions be handled? {kind: question} ^q-multivalued-functions
    - Rather than arbitrarily cutting the plane, Riemann lets the function determine its own domain as a Riemann surface. [§8.1](r2r:chapter_08#81-黎曼曲面概念)
  - [ ] Why does the 3g-3 moduli count fail for g = 0, 1? {kind: question} ^q-moduli-count-anomaly
    - Counting continuous self-transformations s alongside moduli m restores the uniform formula m - s = 3g - 3. [§8.4](r2r:chapter_08#84-紧黎曼曲面的亏格)
  - [ ] Why does holomorphic structure show up in macroscopic physics? {kind: question} ^q-complex-structure-macroscopic
    - Complex structure is basic at quantum scales but mostly buried in large systems; idealized airflow is a lucky exception. [§8.5](r2r:chapter_08#85-黎曼映射定理)

- [ ] 9. Fourier decomposition and hyperfunctions {kind: chapter} ^ch09
  - Recasts Fourier series as Laurent series on the Riemann sphere, derives the positive/negative frequency splitting and the Fourier transform, and builds Sato's hyperfunctions from holomorphic functions. [Chapter 9](r2r:chapter_09)
  - [ ] Fourier series {kind: math} ^fourier-series
    - Decomposition of a periodic function into pure sine and cosine harmonics, compactly written with complex exponentials. [§9.1](r2r:chapter_09#91-傅里叶级数-153)
  - [ ] Laurent series {kind: math} ^laurent-series
    - Power series allowing negative powers; with z = e^(i chi) it is exactly a function's Fourier series on the unit circle. [§9.1](r2r:chapter_09#91-傅里叶级数-153)
  - [ ] Annulus of convergence {kind: math} ^annulus-of-convergence
    - Open ring between two concentric circles where a Laurent series converges; for non-analytic f it shrinks to the unit circle. [§9.2](r2r:chapter_09#92-圆上的函数)
  - [ ] Positive/negative frequency splitting {kind: math} ^frequency-splitting
    - Splitting a function on the circle into parts extending holomorphically to each hemisphere of the Riemann sphere; crucial for quantum theory. [§9.3](r2r:chapter_09#93-黎曼球面上的频率剖分)
  - [ ] Fourier transform {kind: math} ^fourier-transform
    - Limit of a Fourier series as the period goes to infinity, giving the symmetric transform pair f(chi) and g(p). [§9.4](r2r:chapter_09#94-傅里叶变换)
  - [ ] Hyperfunctions {kind: math} ^hyperfunctions
    - Sato's generalized functions: the 'jump' between holomorphic functions on either side of a curve, defined as pairs modulo holomorphic functions. [§9.7](r2r:chapter_09#97-超函数)
  - [ ] Excision theorem {kind: math} ^excision-theorem
    - Guarantees the hyperfunction concept is independent of the chosen open region containing the curve. [§9.7](r2r:chapter_09#97-超函数)
  - [ ] Distributions {kind: math} ^distributions
    - C^-infinity generalized functions dual to C^infinity functions; like the delta function, all are special hyperfunctions. [§9.7](r2r:chapter_09#97-超函数)
  - [ ] Which kind of function is appropriate? {kind: question} ^q-appropriate-functions
    - Are Euler's holomorphic functions or the general modern functions the 'real' ones? Hyperfunctions, built from holomorphic ones, reconcile both. [§9.6](r2r:chapter_09#96-哪种函数是适当的)
  - [ ] How can rigid analytic functions describe unpredictable signals? {kind: question} ^q-rigidity-vs-signals
    - Analytic signals are fixed by any small piece, so wave propagation and information transfer need non-analytic, e.g. discontinuous, functions. [§9.1](r2r:chapter_09#91-傅里叶级数-153)

- [ ] 10. Surfaces {kind: chapter} ^ch10
  - Treats a surface as a real two-dimensional smooth manifold, introducing partial derivatives, vector fields and 1-forms, and recovers holomorphy through the Cauchy-Riemann and Laplace equations. [Chapter 10](r2r:chapter_10)
  - [ ] Complex conjugation {kind: math} ^complex-conjugation
    - The map z = x+iy to z̄ = x−iy, a reflection in the real axis; a non-holomorphic operation that lets x and y be separated. [§10.1](r2r:chapter_10#101-复维和实维-179)
  - [ ] Complex versus real dimension {kind: math} ^complex-vs-real-dimension
    - A Riemann surface is one complex dimension but two real dimensions; admitting z̄ turns functions F(z, z̄) into general functions of x and y. [§10.1](r2r:chapter_10#101-复维和实维-179)
  - [ ] Partial derivatives {kind: math} ^partial-derivatives
    - Derivatives ∂f/∂x, ∂f/∂y taken with the other variable held fixed; their continuity defines smoothness of a function of several variables. [§10.2](r2r:chapter_10#102-光滑偏导数)
  - [ ] Equality of mixed partial derivatives {kind: math} ^mixed-partial-derivatives
    - For C² functions ∂²f/∂x∂y = ∂²f/∂y∂x; exercises show functions smooth in each variable separately need not be jointly smooth. [§10.2](r2r:chapter_10#102-光滑偏导数)
  - [ ] Scalar fields {kind: math} ^scalar-fields
    - A smooth function Φ from a manifold to ℝ or ℂ, distinguished from its coordinate expression f(x, y) in a particular patch. [§10.2](r2r:chapter_10#102-光滑偏导数)
  - [ ] Transition functions {kind: math} ^transition-functions
    - Smooth maps X(x,y), Y(x,y) relating coordinates on overlapping patches, which make smoothness on a manifold independent of coordinates. [§10.2](r2r:chapter_10#102-光滑偏导数)
  - [ ] Chain rule {kind: math} ^chain-rule
    - Expresses ∂/∂x in new coordinates as (∂X/∂x)∂/∂X + (∂Y/∂x)∂/∂Y, showing how derivative operators transform between patches. [§10.3](r2r:chapter_10#103-矢量场和-1-形式)
  - [ ] Vector fields {kind: math} ^vector-fields
    - Differential operators ξ = a∂/∂x + b∂/∂y, pictured as a field of little arrows; ξ(Φ) is the rate of increase of Φ along them. [§10.3](r2r:chapter_10#103-矢量场和-1-形式)
  - [ ] 1-forms {kind: math} ^one-forms
    - Quantities like dΦ = u dx + v dy, not infinitesimals but objects that pair with vector fields; also called covectors, dual to vectors. [§10.3](r2r:chapter_10#103-矢量场和-1-形式)
  - [ ] Gradient dΦ {kind: math} ^gradient
    - The coordinate-free derivative dΦ of a scalar field, pictured by contour lines on a map; ξ(Φ)=0 along the contours. [§10.3](r2r:chapter_10#103-矢量场和-1-形式)
  - [ ] Components and scalar product {kind: math} ^scalar-product
    - Vector components (a,b) and 1-form components (u,v) combine into the scalar product dΦ·ξ = au + bv = ξ(Φ). [Chapter 10](r2r:chapter_10)
  - [ ] Cauchy-Riemann equations {kind: math} ^cauchy-riemann-equations
    - Holomorphy written as ∂Φ/∂z̄ = 0, i.e. ∂α/∂x = ∂β/∂y and ∂α/∂y = −∂β/∂x; a Riemann surface is a real 2-manifold with this structure. [§10.5](r2r:chapter_10#105-柯西黎曼方程)
  - [ ] Laplace equation and Laplacian {kind: math} ^laplace-equation
    - Real and imaginary parts of holomorphic functions satisfy ∇²α = 0 (soap-film picture); used to prove the hyperfunction splitting into positive and negative frequencies. [§10.5](r2r:chapter_10#105-柯西黎曼方程)
  - [ ] Second fundamental confusion of calculus {kind: question} ^q-second-fundamental-confusion
    - Why ∂/∂X ≠ ∂/∂x although X = x, while ∂/∂Y = ∂/∂y although Y ≠ y; resolved geometrically by vector-field arrows. [§10.3](r2r:chapter_10#103-矢量场和-1-形式)
  - [ ] Is a Riemann surface a curve or a surface? {kind: question} ^q-riemann-surface-curve-or-surface
    - Penrose favours the purely holomorphic view of a complex curve, but the real Cauchy-Riemann view gives powerful PDE techniques. [§10.1](r2r:chapter_10#101-复维和实维-179)

- [ ] 11. Hypercomplex numbers {kind: chapter} ^ch11
  - Introduces quaternions, octonions, Clifford and Grassmann algebras as higher-dimensional generalisations of complex numbers, with spinors arising from the geometry of rotations. [Chapter 11](r2r:chapter_11)
  - [ ] Quaternions {kind: math} ^quaternions
    - Hamilton's numbers q = t+ui+vj+wk with i²=j²=k²=ijk=−1; all algebraic laws hold except commutativity of multiplication. [§11.1](r2r:chapter_11#111-四元数代数)
  - [ ] Ring {kind: math} ^ring
    - Algebraic structure with associative addition and multiplication obeying distributive laws; with a unit element 1 it is a ring with unity. [§11.1](r2r:chapter_11#111-四元数代数)
  - [ ] Vector space {kind: math} ^vector-space
    - Set of elements with commutative, associative addition and scalar multiplication; quaternions form a 4-dimensional real vector space with basis 1,i,j,k. [§11.1](r2r:chapter_11#111-四元数代数)
  - [ ] Division algebra {kind: math} ^division-algebra
    - An algebra where every nonzero element has an inverse; for quaternions q⁻¹ = q̄(q̄q)⁻¹ using the quaternionic conjugate. [§11.1](r2r:chapter_11#111-四元数代数)
  - [ ] Octonions {kind: math} ^octonions
    - Graves and Cayley's 8-dimensional non-associative division algebra with seven imaginary units; multiplication table deferred to §16.2. [§11.2](r2r:chapter_11#112-四元数的物理角色)
  - [ ] Hurwitz theorem on division algebras {kind: math} ^hurwitz-theorem
    - Sum-of-squares identities exist only in dimensions 1, 2, 4, 8, so ℝ, ℂ, ℍ and 𝕆 are the only division algebras. [§11.2](r2r:chapter_11#112-四元数的物理角色)
  - [ ] Quaternions as rotations {kind: math} ^quaternion-rotations
    - i, j, k act as π-rotations about three perpendicular axes; i² = −1 only makes sense for spinorial objects, demonstrated with a book and belt. [§11.3](r2r:chapter_11#113-四元数几何)
  - [ ] Spinors {kind: math} ^spinors
    - Objects that change sign under a 2π rotation and return after 4π; the belt trick shows only the parity of 2π turns matters. [§11.3](r2r:chapter_11#113-四元数几何)
  - [ ] Composition of rotations via arcs {kind: math} ^rotation-composition
    - Hamilton's triangle rule on the sphere: rotations as great-circle arcs of half the rotation angle compose like vectors. [§11.4](r2r:chapter_11#114-转动如何叠加)
  - [ ] Clifford algebra {kind: math} ^clifford-algebra
    - Generated by reflections γ₁…γₙ with γᵣ² = −1 and anticommuting; a 2ⁿ-dimensional algebra that contains quaternions and defines spinors. [§11.5](r2r:chapter_11#115-克利福德代数)
  - [ ] Reduced spinors and chirality {kind: math} ^reduced-spinors-chirality
    - In even n the 2^{n/2}-dimensional spin space splits into two half-spinor spaces of opposite handedness, two-dimensional in 4D spacetime. [§11.5](r2r:chapter_11#115-克利福德代数)
  - [ ] Grassmann algebra {kind: math} ^grassmann-algebra
    - Degenerate Clifford algebra with anticommuting generators squaring to zero; needs no metric and describes plane elements of all dimensions. [§11.6](r2r:chapter_11#116-格拉斯曼代数)
  - [ ] Wedge product {kind: math} ^wedge-product
    - Anticommuting product a∧b representing an oriented plane element with magnitude; P∧Q = ±Q∧P by parity of ranks, giving a graded algebra. [§11.6](r2r:chapter_11#116-格拉斯曼代数)
  - [ ] Antisymmetrization bracket notation {kind: math} ^antisymmetrization
    - Square brackets A_[pq] = ½(A_pq − A_qp) and their r-index analogues give the components of wedge products. [§11.6](r2r:chapter_11#116-格拉斯曼代数)
  - [ ] Why did quaternions fail as the language of physics? {kind: question} ^q-why-quaternions-failed
    - Hamilton's quaternion calculus lost out: their 4 dimensions do not fit spacetime signature and there is no good quaternionic holomorphy. [§11.2](r2r:chapter_11#112-四元数的物理角色)
  - [ ] Why does nature distinguish left from right? {kind: question} ^q-parity-violation
    - Nature treats the two reduced spin spaces differently, so some processes are not reflection-invariant (Lee, Yang, Wu). [§11.5](r2r:chapter_11#115-克利福德代数)

- [ ] 12. Manifolds of n dimensions {kind: chapter} ^ch12
  - Builds general n-manifolds from coordinate patches and develops vectors, covectors, p-forms, Cartan's exterior calculus, tensors and complex manifolds. [Chapter 12](r2r:chapter_12)
  - [ ] Configuration space {kind: physics} ^configuration-space
    - Space whose points are the possible placements of a system; a rigid body's is a 6-dimensional non-Euclidean manifold, a gas's 3N-dimensional. [§12.1](r2r:chapter_12#121-为什么要研究高维流形)
  - [ ] Phase space {kind: physics} ^phase-space
    - Space of positions and momenta of all particles, 6N-dimensional; vastly many dimensions even for a little air. [§12.1](r2r:chapter_12#121-为什么要研究高维流形)
  - [ ] Simple and multiple connectedness {kind: math} ^simply-connected
    - A space is simply connected if every loop shrinks to a point; rotation space has a non-shrinkable 2π loop whose double shrinks (topological torsion). [§12.1](r2r:chapter_12#121-为什么要研究高维流形)
  - [ ] n-manifolds and coordinate patches {kind: math} ^n-manifolds
    - Manifolds glued from open regions of ℝⁿ with coordinates x¹…xⁿ and consistent transition functions, required to be Hausdorff; coordinates are then forgotten. [§12.2](r2r:chapter_12#122-流形与坐标拼块)
  - [ ] Tangent space {kind: math} ^tangent-space
    - The n-dimensional vector space of vectors at a point, the limit of ever-higher magnification of its neighbourhood; vector fields are derivations on scalar fields. [§12.3](r2r:chapter_12#123-标量矢量和余矢量)
  - [ ] Covectors and cotangent space {kind: math} ^covectors
    - Linear maps from vectors to scalars, forming the dual cotangent space; a covector defines an (n−1)-plane element, twisted unless it is a gradient. [§12.3](r2r:chapter_12#123-标量矢量和余矢量)
  - [ ] p-forms and multivectors {kind: math} ^p-forms
    - Antisymmetric wedge products of covectors (and of vectors, giving bivectors etc.) describing (n−p)-plane and p-plane elements; forms behave as densities. [§12.4](r2r:chapter_12#124-格拉斯曼积)
  - [ ] Integration of forms {kind: math} ^integration-of-forms
    - In Cartan's exterior calculus a p-form is the natural integrand over a p-dimensional region, invariant under change of coordinates. [§12.5](r2r:chapter_12#125-形式的积分)
  - [ ] Exterior derivative {kind: math} ^exterior-derivative
    - Operator d taking p-forms to (p+1)-forms, defined by axioms or antisymmetrized partial derivatives; it satisfies d² = 0. [§12.6](r2r:chapter_12#126-外导数)
  - [ ] Poincaré lemma {kind: math} ^poincare-lemma
    - A form with dα = 0 is locally exact, α = dβ; e.g. a closed 1-form is locally a gradient. [§12.6](r2r:chapter_12#126-外导数)
  - [ ] Fundamental theorem of exterior calculus {kind: math} ^stokes-theorem
    - ∫_R dφ = ∫_∂R φ, unifying Gauss, Green, Kelvin-Stokes and the fundamental theorem of calculus; Penrose avoids the name 'Stokes theorem'. [§12.6](r2r:chapter_12#126-外导数)
  - [ ] Compactness {kind: math} ^compactness
    - A region is compact if every infinite sequence in it accumulates at a point of it; sphere, torus and closed disc are compact. [§12.6](r2r:chapter_12#126-外导数)
  - [ ] Orientation and boundary {kind: math} ^orientability
    - A consistent handedness at every point (the Möbius strip has none); boundaries of oriented regions obey ∂² = 0, complementing d² = 0. [§12.6](r2r:chapter_12#126-外导数)
  - [ ] Volume element and duality {kind: math} ^volume-element
    - An n-form ε measuring n-dimensional density; contracting with it converts (n−p)-vectors into p-forms (a Hodge-type duality). [§12.7](r2r:chapter_12#127-体积元求和规则)
  - [ ] Einstein summation convention {kind: math} ^summation-convention
    - A repeated upper and lower index implies summation, so the scalar product is written β_r ξ^r; contraction generalizes it. [§12.7](r2r:chapter_12#127-体积元求和规则)
  - [ ] Tensors {kind: math} ^tensors
    - A [p q]-valent tensor is a multilinear function of p vectors and q covectors, written with q upper and p lower indices. [§12.8](r2r:chapter_12#128-张量抽象指标记法和图示记法)
  - [ ] Abstract-index and diagrammatic notation {kind: math} ^abstract-index-notation
    - Penrose's indices as abstract labels, not coordinate values, plus diagrams of shapes with arms and legs where contraction is joining lines. [§12.8](r2r:chapter_12#128-张量抽象指标记法和图示记法)
  - [ ] Complex manifolds {kind: math} ^complex-manifolds
    - Manifolds patched holomorphically from ℂⁿ, or real 2n-manifolds with an almost complex structure J (J² = −1), integrable by Newlander-Nirenberg. [§12.9](r2r:chapter_12#129-复流形)
  - [ ] Why study manifolds of many dimensions? {kind: question} ^q-why-higher-dimensions
    - Spacetime is 4-dimensional, yet configuration and phase spaces of physical systems, and theories like strings, need many more dimensions. [§12.1](r2r:chapter_12#121-为什么要研究高维流形)
  - [ ] Mathematicians' versus physicists' notation {kind: question} ^q-notation-conflict
    - Coordinate-free β·ξ versus component β_r ξ^r: Penrose argues the conflict is largely artificial and resolves it with abstract indices. [§12.8](r2r:chapter_12#128-张量抽象指标记法和图示记法)

- [ ] 13. Symmetry groups {kind: chapter} ^ch13
  - A crash course in group theory: finite and continuous groups, matrices, eigenvalues, representations and Lie algebras, and the orthogonal, unitary and symplectic classical groups. [Chapter 13](r2r:chapter_13)
  - [ ] Groups {kind: math} ^groups
    - A set with associative multiplication, identity and inverses, modelled on symmetries of a square; abelian if multiplication commutes. [§13.1](r2r:chapter_13#131-变换群)
  - [ ] Rotation groups SO(3) and O(3) {kind: math} ^so3
    - Rotation symmetries of a sphere form the 3-manifold SO(3); including reflections gives the disconnected two-component group O(3). [§13.1](r2r:chapter_13#131-变换群)
  - [ ] Subgroups, normal subgroups and quotient groups {kind: math} ^normal-subgroups
    - A normal subgroup S satisfies Sg = gS for all g, so its cosets form the quotient group G/S; relevant to symmetry breaking. [§13.2](r2r:chapter_13#132-子群和单群)
  - [ ] Simple groups and their classification {kind: math} ^simple-groups
    - Groups with no nontrivial normal subgroup; Killing-Cartan classified simple Lie groups (A, B, C, D, five exceptional), and finite ones include the Monster. [§13.2](r2r:chapter_13#132-子群和单群)
  - [ ] Linear transformations and matrices {kind: math} ^matrices
    - Linear maps x^a ↦ T^a_b x^b preserving vector-space structure, as arrays with non-commutative multiplication, Kronecker delta identity and inverses when non-singular. [§13.3](r2r:chapter_13#133-线性变换和矩阵)
  - [ ] General and special linear groups {kind: math} ^general-linear-group
    - GL(n) is the group of non-singular n×n matrices, real or complex; SL(n) is its normal subgroup of unit-determinant elements. [§13.3](r2r:chapter_13#133-线性变换和矩阵)
  - [ ] Determinant {kind: math} ^determinant
    - Number built from a matrix with Levi-Civita symbols, zero exactly when the matrix is singular, with det(AB) = det A det B. [§13.4](r2r:chapter_13#134-行列式和迹)
  - [ ] Trace {kind: math} ^trace
    - Sum of diagonal elements; det(I+εA) = 1+ε trace A links infinitesimal SL(n) elements to traceless matrices, and det e^A = e^{trace A}. [§13.4](r2r:chapter_13#134-行列式和迹)
  - [ ] Eigenvalues and eigenvectors {kind: math} ^eigenvectors
    - Tv = λv where det(T − λI) = 0; a basis of eigenvectors makes T diagonal, a canonical form vital to quantum theory. [§13.5](r2r:chapter_13#135-本征值与本征矢量)
  - [ ] Group representations {kind: math} ^representation-theory
    - Homomorphisms g ↦ T(g) into GL(n) preserving multiplication, faithful if injective; central to quantum theory and particle physics. [§13.6](r2r:chapter_13#136-表示理论与李代数)
  - [ ] Lie groups and Lie algebras {kind: math} ^lie-algebra
    - Infinitesimal elements I+εA of a continuous group with commutator [A,B], antisymmetry, Jacobi identity and structure constants fix its local structure. [§13.6](r2r:chapter_13#136-表示理论与李代数)
  - [ ] Reducible and irreducible representations {kind: math} ^reducibility
    - Tensor representation spaces split, e.g. into symmetric and antisymmetric parts, as direct sums; compact semisimple groups (non-singular Killing form) are completely reducible. [§13.7](r2r:chapter_13#137-张量表示空间可约性)
  - [ ] Metric and signature {kind: math} ^metric
    - A symmetric non-singular tensor g_ab defining lengths via ds² = g_ab dx^a dx^b; diagonalizes to ±1 entries giving its signature (p,q). [§13.8](r2r:chapter_13#138-正交群)
  - [ ] Orthogonal groups {kind: math} ^orthogonal-group
    - O(n), SO(n) preserve a positive-definite metric; pseudo-orthogonal O(p,q) include the Lorentz group O(1,3), all real forms of O(n,ℂ). [§13.8](r2r:chapter_13#138-正交群)
  - [ ] Hermitian forms and conjugation {kind: math} ^hermitian-conjugation
    - Hermitian-symmetric forms, antilinear in one slot, give the operation * (conjugate transpose), Hermitian matrices and the Hermitian scalar product and norm. [§13.9](r2r:chapter_13#139-酉群)
  - [ ] Unitary groups {kind: math} ^unitary-group
    - Complex linear maps preserving a Hermitian form, T⁻¹ = T*; U(n) for positive-definite, pseudo-unitary U(p,q) otherwise, SU(n) with unit determinant. [§13.9](r2r:chapter_13#139-酉群)
  - [ ] Symplectic groups {kind: math} ^symplectic-group
    - Groups preserving an antisymmetric bilinear form in even dimensions, vital to classical mechanics; low-dimensional coincidences like SU(2) = Sp(1), locally SO(3). [§13.10](r2r:chapter_13#1310-辛群)
  - [ ] Why are physical symmetries exact? {kind: question} ^q-exact-symmetry
    - Physical symmetric objects seem only approximate, yet all known interactions rest on exact symmetry via gauge connections, developed in later chapters. [§13.1](r2r:chapter_13#131-变换群)
  - [ ] Is there a broken fundamental symmetry group? {kind: question} ^q-broken-symmetry
    - Particle physics hopes the observed symmetries are subgroups left after breaking of a hypothetical fundamental group, which is still not found. [§13.2](r2r:chapter_13#132-子群和单群)
  - [ ] When do real forms and signatures matter? {kind: question} ^q-real-forms
    - Groups O(p,q) share one complexification; physicists often apply compact-case results to non-compact ones, and quantum theory blurs signatures. [§13.8](r2r:chapter_13#138-正交群)

- [ ] 14. Calculus on manifolds {kind: chapter} ^ch14
  - Equips smooth manifolds with connections, curvature, metrics and symplectic forms so that tensor fields can be differentiated and geometry measured. [Chapter 14](r2r:chapter_14)
  - [ ] Affine space {kind: math} ^affine-space
    - A vector space that has 'forgotten' its origin, like Euclidean space: parallelograms make sense but no point is preferred. [§14.1](r2r:chapter_14#141-流形上的微分)
  - [ ] Parallel transport {kind: math} ^parallel-transport
    - Carrying a tangent vector along a curve while keeping it 'parallel'; on the sphere the result depends on the path taken. [§14.2](r2r:chapter_14#142-平行移动)
  - [ ] Covariant derivative (connection) {kind: math} ^covariant-derivative
    - The operator ∇ that differentiates vector and tensor fields by comparing them with parallel transport; linear and obeying Leibniz's rule. [§14.3](r2r:chapter_14#143-协变导数)
  - [ ] Torsion tensor {kind: math} ^torsion-tensor
    - Antisymmetric part of ∇a∇b acting on scalars; usually assumed zero in physics, nonzero in Einstein-Cartan and supergravity theories. [§14.4](r2r:chapter_14#144-曲率和挠率)
  - [ ] Curvature tensor {kind: math} ^curvature-tensor
    - Defined by the commutator of covariant derivatives; it measures how a vector changes when parallel-transported round a small loop. [§14.4](r2r:chapter_14#144-曲率和挠率)
  - [ ] Bianchi symmetry and Bianchi identity {kind: math} ^bianchi-identity
    - R_[abc]^d = 0 and ∇_[a R_bc]d^e = 0 in the torsion-free case; the latter is crucial for Einstein's field equations. [§14.4](r2r:chapter_14#144-曲率和挠率)
  - [ ] Geodesics and affine parameters {kind: math} ^geodesics
    - Curves whose tangent is parallel-transported along themselves, the analogue of straight lines; an affine parameter keeps the tangent's scaling constant. [§14.5](r2r:chapter_14#145-测地线平行四边形和曲率)
  - [ ] Geodesic parallelograms and curvature {kind: math} ^geodesic-parallelogram
    - Small parallelograms built from geodesics fail to close at O(ε²) with torsion, O(ε³) without; transport round them reads off R. [§14.5](r2r:chapter_14#145-测地线平行四边形和曲率)
  - [ ] Geodesic deviation {kind: math} ^geodesic-deviation
    - Relative acceleration of neighbouring geodesics governed by curvature: positive curvature brings them together, negative curvature spreads them apart. [§14.5](r2r:chapter_14#145-测地线平行四边形和曲率)
  - [ ] Lie bracket of vector fields {kind: math} ^lie-bracket
    - The commutator [ξ,η] of two vector fields is again a vector field, measuring the gap of their quadrilateral; it obeys the Jacobi identity. [§14.6](r2r:chapter_14#146-李导数)
  - [ ] Lie derivative {kind: math} ^lie-derivative
    - Derivative of a tensor field relative to its drag along a given vector field; connection-independent, with £ξη = [ξ,η]. [§14.6](r2r:chapter_14#146-李导数)
  - [ ] (Pseudo-)Riemannian manifold {kind: math} ^riemannian-manifold
    - A manifold with a metric g_ab giving lengths of curves ∫ds; Lorentzian signature +--- matters for relativity. [§14.7](r2r:chapter_14#147-度规能为你做什么)
  - [ ] Levi-Civita (Riemannian, Christoffel) connection {kind: math} ^levi-civita-connection
    - The unique torsion-free connection with ∇g = 0, preserving lengths under parallel transport; its geodesics extremize length. [§14.7](r2r:chapter_14#147-度规能为你做什么)
  - [ ] Riemann(-Christoffel) tensor {kind: math} ^riemann-tensor
    - Curvature tensor with index lowered by g, R_abcd = -R_abdc = R_cdab, having n²(n²-1)/12 independent components. [§14.7](r2r:chapter_14#147-度规能为你做什么)
  - [ ] Killing vectors {kind: math} ^killing-vectors
    - Vector fields κ with £κ g = 0, i.e. ∇(a κb) = 0; they generate continuous symmetries of a (pseudo-)Riemannian manifold. [§14.7](r2r:chapter_14#147-度规能为你做什么)
  - [ ] Symplectic manifolds {kind: math} ^symplectic-manifolds
    - Manifolds with a closed nonsingular antisymmetric form S_ab (dS = 0); locally all alike, a 'flabby' structure with no curvature. [§14.8](r2r:chapter_14#148-辛流形)
  - [ ] Poisson bracket {kind: math} ^poisson-bracket
    - Combines two scalar fields via the inverse symplectic form; antisymmetric and satisfying the Jacobi identity, key to Hamiltonian mechanics. [§14.8](r2r:chapter_14#148-辛流形)
  - [ ] Does physical spacetime have torsion? {kind: question} ^q-spacetime-torsion
    - Most physical theories take ∇ torsion-free, but spin/torsion theories and supergravity allow torsion with physical meaning. [§14.4](r2r:chapter_14#144-曲率和挠率)
  - [ ] Which geometric structures are 'flabby'? {kind: question} ^q-flabby-structures
    - Symplectic and complex structures have no local invariants unlike metrics; the book flags this issue as relevant to twistor theory. [§14.8](r2r:chapter_14#148-辛流形)

- [ ] 15. Fibre bundles and gauge connections {kind: chapter} ^ch15
  - Introduces fibre bundles, their cross-sections and connections, showing how bundle curvature underlies the gauge fields of modern particle physics. [Chapter 15](r2r:chapter_15)
  - [ ] Kaluza-Klein extra dimensions {kind: physics} ^kaluza-klein
    - The idea, pictured as a hosepipe, that 'internal' dimensions are tiny extra spatial dimensions; the book treats it as speculative. [§15.1](r2r:chapter_15#151-纤维丛的物理背景)
  - [ ] Fibre bundles {kind: math} ^fibre-bundles
    - A manifold B over base M, locally like the product M×V with fibre V but possibly twisted globally, as in the Möbius bundle. [§15.2](r2r:chapter_15#152-丛的数学思想)
  - [ ] Möbius bundle {kind: math} ^mobius-bundle
    - Twisted real line bundle over a circle, contrasted with the trivial cylinder; the twist comes from the fibre's sign-reversal symmetry. [§15.2](r2r:chapter_15#152-丛的数学思想)
  - [ ] Cross-sections of bundles {kind: math} ^bundle-cross-sections
    - Continuous images of the base meeting each fibre once, generalizing function graphs; every Möbius-bundle section crosses the zero section. [§15.3](r2r:chapter_15#153-丛的截面)
  - [ ] Clifford bundle (Hopf fibration) {kind: math} ^hopf-fibration
    - S³ fibred by circles over S², built from complex lines Aw+Bz=0 in C²; it has no continuous cross-section. [§15.4](r2r:chapter_15#154-克利福德丛)
  - [ ] Clifford parallels {kind: math} ^clifford-parallels
    - The fibre circles of the Hopf fibration: great circles in S³ that link one another yet stay equidistant. [§15.4](r2r:chapter_15#154-克利福德丛)
  - [ ] Complex vector bundles {kind: math} ^complex-vector-bundles
    - Bundles whose fibres are complex vector spaces; the complex line bundle over CP¹ has sections representing spinor fields on S². [§15.5](r2r:chapter_15#155-复矢量丛余切丛)
  - [ ] Tangent bundle {kind: math} ^tangent-bundle
    - T(M), whose points are a point of M with a tangent vector there; its cross-sections are vector fields. [§15.5](r2r:chapter_15#155-复矢量丛余切丛)
  - [ ] Cotangent bundle {kind: math} ^cotangent-bundle
    - T*(M), built from covectors; it is naturally a 2n-dimensional symplectic manifold, the phase space of classical mechanics. [§15.5](r2r:chapter_15#155-复矢量丛余切丛)
  - [ ] Projective space {kind: math} ^projective-space
    - Space of lines through the origin of an (n+1)-dimensional vector space, adding points at infinity so parallels meet; real or complex. [§15.6](r2r:chapter_15#156-射影空间)
  - [ ] Homogeneous coordinates {kind: math} ^homogeneous-coordinates
    - Ratios z⁰:z¹:…:zⁿ, not all zero, labelling points of RPⁿ or CPⁿ. [§15.6](r2r:chapter_15#156-射影空间)
  - [ ] Pappus and Desargues theorems {kind: math} ^pappus-desargues-theorems
    - Classic incidence theorems of plane projective geometry, stated cleanly once parallels meet at infinity. [§15.6](r2r:chapter_15#156-射影空间)
  - [ ] Bundle (gauge) connection {kind: math} ^gauge-connection
    - Notion of 'constancy' of fibre values along curves in the base; on a 'strained' bundle over S¹ it is path-dependent. [§15.7](r2r:chapter_15#157-丛联络的非平凡性)
  - [ ] Bundle curvature {kind: math} ^bundle-curvature
    - Commutator of bundle covariant derivatives ∇a = ∂/∂xᵃ - Aa, giving F_ab; physical gauge fields like Maxwell's appear as such curvature. [§15.8](r2r:chapter_15#158-丛曲率)
  - [ ] Are internal dimensions real extra spatial dimensions? {kind: question} ^q-extra-dimensions
    - Kaluza-Klein, supergravity and string theory treat them as tiny extra dimensions; the book prefers viewing them as bundle fibres. [§15.1](r2r:chapter_15#151-纤维丛的物理背景)
  - [ ] Where do the fibre symmetries of gauge theories come from? {kind: question} ^q-gauge-symmetry-origin
    - Gauge theories need exact fibre symmetries, raising the fundamental question of their origin and nature, revisited in chapters 28, 31, 34. [§15.8](r2r:chapter_15#158-丛曲率)

- [ ] 16. The ladder of infinity {kind: chapter} ^ch16
  - Contrasts finite number systems and geometries with Cantor's hierarchy of infinities, then examines the foundational puzzles of set theory, computability and Gödel's theorem. [Chapter 16](r2r:chapter_16)
  - [ ] Finite fields {kind: math} ^finite-fields
    - Number systems F_p (integers mod a prime) and F_q with q = p^m, where adding p equal elements gives zero. [§16.1](r2r:chapter_16#161-有限域)
  - [ ] Finite projective geometry {kind: math} ^finite-projective-geometry
    - Projective spaces Pⁿ(F_q) with 1+q+…+qⁿ points; 'magic disc' constructions realize finite versions of Pappus and Desargues. [§16.2](r2r:chapter_16#162-物理上需要的是有限还是无限几何)
  - [ ] Fano plane {kind: math} ^fano-plane
    - The 7-point projective plane over F_2, whose lines encode the multiplication table of the octonion generators. [§16.2](r2r:chapter_16#162-物理上需要的是有限还是无限几何)
  - [ ] Cardinality and cardinal numbers {kind: math} ^cardinality
    - Sets have equal cardinality when in one-to-one correspondence; naturals, integers and rationals all share the countable cardinality ℵ₀. [§16.3](r2r:chapter_16#163-无限的不同大小)
  - [ ] Axiom of choice {kind: math} ^axiom-of-choice
    - For any set of nonempty sets there is a set picking one element from each; it implies all cardinals are comparable. [§16.3](r2r:chapter_16#163-无限的不同大小)
  - [ ] Cantor's diagonal argument {kind: math} ^cantor-diagonal-argument
    - Proof by contradiction that α < 2^α for every cardinal, so the reals (C = 2^ℵ₀) outnumber the natural numbers. [§16.4](r2r:chapter_16#164-康托尔对角线法)
  - [ ] Continuum hypothesis {kind: math} ^continuum-hypothesis
    - Cantor's conjecture that 2^ℵ₀ = ℵ₁, no cardinal lying between; shown independent of standard set-theory axioms (Gödel, Cohen). [§16.5](r2r:chapter_16#165-数学基础方面的难题)
  - [ ] Russell paradox {kind: math} ^russell-paradox
    - The set of all sets not members of themselves is contradictory; Cantor's argument applied to 'the set of all sets', prompting the set/class distinction. [§16.5](r2r:chapter_16#165-数学基础方面的难题)
  - [ ] Turing machines and the universal Turing machine {kind: math} ^turing-machines
    - Idealized algorithms acting on natural numbers, coded by numbers; a universal machine U simulates any Turing machine. [§16.6](r2r:chapter_16#166-图灵机和哥德尔定理)
  - [ ] Halting problem (non-computability) {kind: math} ^halting-problem
    - Turing's diagonal proof that no algorithm decides whether a Turing machine halts; the set of good machines is not recursively enumerable. [§16.6](r2r:chapter_16#166-图灵机和哥德尔定理)
  - [ ] Recursive and recursively enumerable sets {kind: math} ^recursive-sets
    - Subsets of ℕ whose membership, or whose listing, is given by an algorithm; the 'constructivist' notion of an acceptable set. [§16.6](r2r:chapter_16#166-图灵机和哥德尔定理)
  - [ ] Gödel's incompleteness theorem {kind: math} ^godel-incompleteness
    - Any sound formal system for arithmetic fails to prove some true Π₁ sentences; derived here from Turing's halting result. [§16.6](r2r:chapter_16#166-图灵机和哥德尔定理)
  - [ ] Functional freedom of fields {kind: physics} ^functional-freedom
    - Cardinality fails to size physical spaces; field freedom is gauged as ∞^(k∞ⁿ), k components of functions of n variables. [§16.7](r2r:chapter_16#167-物理学中无限的大小)
  - [ ] Does physics need finite or infinite geometry? {kind: question} ^q-finite-or-infinite-physics
    - Could the real continuum approximate a finite number system? The book argues infinite structures, at least for time, must be taken seriously. [§16.2](r2r:chapter_16#162-物理上需要的是有限还是无限几何)
  - [ ] Is the continuum hypothesis true? {kind: question} ^q-continuum-hypothesis-truth
    - Independent of standard axioms, its truth may be objective or merely a matter of chosen mathematical standards. [§16.5](r2r:chapter_16#165-数学基础方面的难题)
  - [ ] Does Gödel's theorem imply unknowable mathematical truths? {kind: question} ^q-godel-unknowable-truths
    - The book rejects this popular reading: the Gödel sentence is seen to be true, so insight transcends any formal system. [§16.6](r2r:chapter_16#166-图灵机和哥德尔定理)

- [ ] 17. Spacetime {kind: chapter} ^ch17
  - Traces spacetime structure from Aristotelian and Galilean to Newton-Cartan, Minkowski and Einstein spacetimes, driven by relativity, equivalence and a constant speed of light. [Chapter 17](r2r:chapter_17)
  - [ ] Aristotelian spacetime {kind: physics} ^aristotelian-spacetime
    - The product E¹×E³ with absolute time and absolute space, where a point of space persists from one moment to the next. [§17.1](r2r:chapter_17#171-亚里士多德物理学的时空)
  - [ ] Galilean relativity principle {kind: physics} ^galilean-relativity
    - Galileo's ship argument: the laws of dynamics are identical in every uniformly moving frame, so absolute space has no meaning. [§17.2](r2r:chapter_17#172-伽利略原理下的时空相对性)
  - [ ] Galilean spacetime {kind: physics} ^galilean-spacetime
    - A fibre bundle with base E¹ (absolute time) and fibre E³, without pointwise identification of spaces at different times. [§17.2](r2r:chapter_17#172-伽利略原理下的时空相对性)
  - [ ] World line {kind: physics} ^world-line
    - The history of a particle as a curve in spacetime; inertial motion appears as straight world lines. [§17.3](r2r:chapter_17#173-时空的牛顿动力学)
  - [ ] Newton's laws and Newtonian dynamics {kind: physics} ^newtons-laws
    - Three laws formulated on Galilean spacetime with its affine structure; forces sum vectorially and act instantaneously between particles. [§17.3](r2r:chapter_17#173-时空的牛顿动力学)
  - [ ] Newton's law of gravitation {kind: physics} ^newtonian-gravity
    - Inverse-square attraction GmM/r² between masses, giving an extraordinarily accurate universal theory from very simple ingredients. [§17.3](r2r:chapter_17#173-时空的牛顿动力学)
  - [ ] Equivalence principle {kind: physics} ^equivalence-principle
    - Equality of gravitational and inertial mass makes a uniform gravitational field equivalent to acceleration; free fall is Einstein's inertial motion. [§17.4](r2r:chapter_17#174-等效原理)
  - [ ] Newton-Cartan spacetime {kind: physics} ^newton-cartan-spacetime
    - Newtonian gravity rebuilt as a bundle over E¹ with a connection whose geodesics are free-fall world lines; curvature encodes gravity. [§17.5](r2r:chapter_17#175-嘉当的牛顿时空)
  - [ ] Tidal effect {kind: physics} ^tidal-effect
    - Relative distortion of nearby free-falling particles, a volume-preserving squash in vacuum, identified with spacetime curvature via geodesic deviation. [§17.5](r2r:chapter_17#175-嘉当的牛顿时空)
  - [ ] Light cones (null cones) {kind: physics} ^light-cones
    - The cone of photon histories through an event, given by g_ab vᵃvᵇ = 0 for a Lorentzian metric; it fixes light speed. [§17.7](r2r:chapter_17#177-光锥)
  - [ ] Abandoning absolute time {kind: physics} ^relativity-of-simultaneity
    - Uniform null cones are incompatible with Galilean E³ slicing, so absolute simultaneity must go; it seems natural only because c is so large. [§17.8](r2r:chapter_17#178-放弃绝对时间)
  - [ ] Minkowski spacetime {kind: physics} ^minkowski-space
    - Flat spacetime of special relativity, with uniformly arranged null cones and a metric of signature +--- whose scale is fixed. [§17.8](r2r:chapter_17#178-放弃绝对时间)
  - [ ] Proper time {kind: physics} ^proper-time
    - τ = ∫ds along a timelike world line, the time an ideal clock measures; spacelike world lines are physically forbidden. [§17.8](r2r:chapter_17#178-放弃绝对时间)
  - [ ] Causal structure (timelike, null, spacelike) {kind: physics} ^causal-structure
    - Null cones determine the future of an event: massive particles move inside cones, light on them, signals never outside. [§17.8](r2r:chapter_17#178-放弃绝对时间)
  - [ ] Einstein's spacetime of general relativity {kind: physics} ^einsteinian-spacetime
    - A curved Lorentzian manifold generalizing Minkowski space as Newton-Cartan generalizes Galilean; its Levi-Civita curvature describes gravity. [§17.9](r2r:chapter_17#179-爱因斯坦广义相对论的时空)
  - [ ] Why spacetime rather than space and time? {kind: question} ^q-why-spacetime
    - The chapter shows that even Galilean relativity denies a persisting absolute space, so a unified spacetime picture is required. [§17.1](r2r:chapter_17#171-亚里士多德物理学的时空)
  - [ ] Is gravity a force or spacetime geometry? {kind: question} ^q-gravity-a-force
    - The equivalence principle makes free fall inertial, so gravity is not felt; its true signature is tidal curvature. [§17.4](r2r:chapter_17#174-等效原理)
  - [ ] How can light speed be constant for all observers? {kind: question} ^q-light-speed-vs-relativity
    - Maxwell's fixed light speed seems to clash with the relativity principle; null-cone geometry resolves it by abandoning absolute time. [§17.6](r2r:chapter_17#176-确定不变的有限光速)
  - [ ] Are closed timelike curves (time travel) physical? {kind: question} ^q-time-travel
    - Some Einstein spacetimes allow causal structures where signals return to their own past; Penrose excludes them as unphysical. [§17.9](r2r:chapter_17#179-爱因斯坦广义相对论的时空)

- [ ] 18. Minkowskian geometry {kind: chapter} ^ch18
  - Develops the geometry of Minkowski spacetime: its Poincaré symmetry, Lorentzian orthogonality and the clock paradox, hyperbolic velocity space, the celestial sphere as a Riemann sphere, and relativistic energy, momentum and angular momentum. [Chapter 18](r2r:chapter_18)
  - [ ] Complexified Minkowski space {kind: math} ^complexified-minkowski-space
    - Euclidean 4-space and Minkowski space appear as different real slices of one complex 4-space with a holomorphic metric. [§18.1](r2r:chapter_18#181-欧几里得型与闵可夫斯基型四维空间)
  - [ ] Poincaré group {kind: physics} ^poincare-group
    - The ten-dimensional symmetry group of Minkowski space: four translations plus the six-dimensional Lorentz group. [§18.2](r2r:chapter_18#182-闵可夫斯基空间的对称群)
  - [ ] Lorentzian orthogonality and simultaneity {kind: physics} ^lorentzian-orthogonality
    - An observer's simultaneous spaces are the Minkowski orthogonal complements of its world-line, tilting with velocity; null directions are self-orthogonal. [§18.3](r2r:chapter_18#183-洛伦兹正交性时钟悖论)
  - [ ] Clock (twin) paradox {kind: physics} ^clock-paradox
    - The reversed Lorentzian triangle inequality shows a travelling twin's broken world-line records less proper time than the stay-at-home's. [§18.3](r2r:chapter_18#183-洛伦兹正交性时钟悖论)
  - [ ] Hyperbolic velocity space {kind: physics} ^relativistic-velocity-space
    - The unit hyperboloid of future timelike directions in Minkowski space has hyperbolic geometry and serves as relativistic velocity space. [§18.4](r2r:chapter_18#184-闵可夫斯基空间的双曲几何)
  - [ ] Rapidity {kind: physics} ^rapidity
    - Hyperbolic distance in velocity space, rho = artanh v; speed of light corresponds to infinite rapidity. [§18.4](r2r:chapter_18#184-闵可夫斯基空间的双曲几何)
  - [ ] Relativistic velocity addition {kind: physics} ^relativistic-velocity-addition
    - Collinear velocities combine as (u+v)/(1+uv) by adding rapidities; general ones by a hyperbolic triangle law. [§18.4](r2r:chapter_18#184-闵可夫斯基空间的双曲几何)
  - [ ] Celestial sphere as a Riemann sphere {kind: physics} ^celestial-sphere
    - The sphere of light rays through an event carries the conformal structure of a Riemann sphere, preserved under change of observer, so circles stay circles. [§18.5](r2r:chapter_18#185-作为黎曼球面的天球)
  - [ ] Stellar aberration {kind: physics} ^stellar-aberration
    - Observer motion distorts apparent star positions, yet the distortion preserves circles and angles on the celestial sphere. [§18.5](r2r:chapter_18#185-作为黎曼球面的天球)
  - [ ] FitzGerald-Lorentz contraction {kind: physics} ^lorentz-contraction
    - Moving bodies are flattened along the motion, but light-travel effects make a moving sphere still look circular, apparently rotated. [§18.5](r2r:chapter_18#185-作为黎曼球面的天球)
  - [ ] Kinetic and potential energy {kind: physics} ^kinetic-energy
    - Newtonian energy splits into kinetic energy mv^2/2 and potential energy stored in forces; the total is conserved. [§18.6](r2r:chapter_18#186-牛顿能量和角动量)
  - [ ] Conservation of momentum {kind: physics} ^momentum-conservation
    - Total momentum p = mv summed over particles is conserved and transforms consistently under Galilean boosts. [§18.6](r2r:chapter_18#186-牛顿能量和角动量)
  - [ ] Angular momentum {kind: physics} ^angular-momentum
    - Moment of momentum x wedge p about an origin, conserved for isolated systems, alongside the centre-of-mass quantity tp - mx. [§18.6](r2r:chapter_18#186-牛顿能量和角动量)
  - [ ] Energy-momentum 4-vector {kind: physics} ^four-momentum
    - Energy and 3-momentum combine into a spacetime vector, additively conserved; for a massive particle it is rest mass times 4-velocity. [§18.7](r2r:chapter_18#187-相对论性能量和角动量)
  - [ ] Mass-energy equivalence E = mc\^2 {kind: physics} ^mass-energy-equivalence
    - The time component of 4-momentum measures both total energy and total mass, which become equivalent via E = mc^2. [§18.7](r2r:chapter_18#187-相对论性能量和角动量)
  - [ ] Rest mass {kind: physics} ^rest-mass
    - Frame-independent Minkowski length of 4-momentum; not conserved, so a massive pion can decay into two massless photons. [§18.7](r2r:chapter_18#187-相对论性能量和角动量)
  - [ ] Relativistic angular momentum {kind: physics} ^relativistic-angular-momentum
    - An antisymmetric tensor M^ab = x^a p^b - x^b p^a unifying angular momentum with centre-of-mass motion, tied to the Lorentz rotations. [§18.7](r2r:chapter_18#187-相对论性能量和角动量)
  - [ ] Is imaginary (Euclidean) time legitimate? {kind: question} ^q-imaginary-time
    - Penrose doubts the old t = iw trick and its modern Euclidean quantum field theory descendants, deferring the critique to later. [§18.1](r2r:chapter_18#181-欧几里得型与闵可夫斯基型四维空间)
  - [ ] Can the Lorentz contraction be seen? {kind: question} ^q-lorentz-contraction-visible
    - Why a rapidly moving sphere still presents a circular outline despite its FitzGerald-Lorentz flattening. [§18.5](r2r:chapter_18#185-作为黎曼球面的天球)
  - [ ] Is mass conserved in relativity? {kind: question} ^q-mass-conservation
    - Energy, momentum and angular momentum survive in relativity, but mass conservation merges with energy and rest mass is not conserved. [§18.6](r2r:chapter_18#186-牛顿能量和角动量)

- [ ] 19. The classical fields of Maxwell and Einstein {kind: chapter} ^ch19
  - Presents Maxwell's electromagnetism in the language of forms, its conservation laws and its reading as gauge curvature, then the energy-momentum tensor, Einstein's field equations, the cosmological constant, the Weyl tensor and the problem of gravitational energy. [Chapter 19](r2r:chapter_19)
  - [ ] Maxwell field tensor {kind: physics} ^maxwell-field-tensor
    - Electric and magnetic fields combine into a spacetime 2-form F, with charge and current forming the charge-current vector J. [§19.2](r2r:chapter_19#192-麦克斯韦电磁场理论)
  - [ ] Hodge dual {kind: math} ^hodge-dual
    - Uses the Levi-Civita tensor to turn a p-form into a (4-p)-form, here swapping the roles of electric and magnetic fields. [§19.2](r2r:chapter_19#192-麦克斯韦电磁场理论)
  - [ ] Maxwell's equations {kind: physics} ^maxwell-equations
    - Written compactly as dF = 0 and d*F = 4 pi *J, they govern the time evolution of electric and magnetic fields. [§19.2](r2r:chapter_19#192-麦克斯韦电磁场理论)
  - [ ] Self-dual and anti-self-dual fields {kind: physics} ^self-dual-field
    - Complex combinations F +/- i*F splitting the Maxwell field into parts that describe right- and left-handed photons. [§19.2](r2r:chapter_19#192-麦克斯韦电磁场理论)
  - [ ] Electromagnetic waves {kind: physics} ^electromagnetic-waves
    - Maxwell's equations admit waves moving at light speed, identifying light as electromagnetic radiation. [§19.2](r2r:chapter_19#192-麦克斯韦电磁场理论)
  - [ ] Conservation of electric charge {kind: physics} ^charge-conservation
    - From d*J = 0 and the fundamental theorem of exterior calculus, total charge flux through any closed 3-surface vanishes; Gauss's law follows similarly. [§19.3](r2r:chapter_19#193-麦克斯韦理论中的守恒律和通量定律)
  - [ ] Electromagnetic potential {kind: physics} ^electromagnetic-potential
    - dF = 0 lets F = dA locally by the Poincaré lemma; A is not locally measurable because A + d Theta is equivalent. [§19.4](r2r:chapter_19#194-作为规范曲率的麦克斯韦场)
  - [ ] Gauge invariance {kind: physics} ^gauge-invariance
    - Phase changes of charged fields compensated by shifts of A; the potential gives a bundle connection whose curvature is the Maxwell field. [§19.4](r2r:chapter_19#194-作为规范曲率的麦克斯韦场)
  - [ ] Weyl's gauge theory {kind: physics} ^weyl-gauge-theory
    - Weyl's 1918 idea of path-dependent length scale (conformal rescaling); it failed physically but works with unit-modulus complex phases. [§19.4](r2r:chapter_19#194-作为规范曲率的麦克斯韦场)
  - [ ] Aharonov-Bohm effect {kind: physics} ^aharonov-bohm-effect
    - Interference fringes shift through a field-free region, depending on the loop integral of A over a topologically non-trivial path. [§19.4](r2r:chapter_19#194-作为规范曲率的麦克斯韦场)
  - [ ] Energy-momentum tensor {kind: physics} ^energy-momentum-tensor
    - Symmetric tensor T_ab giving densities and fluxes of energy and momentum, satisfying the conservation equation div T = 0; the source of gravity. [§19.5](r2r:chapter_19#195-能量动量张量)
  - [ ] Conservation laws from Killing vectors {kind: physics} ^killing-vector-conservation
    - Contracting T_ab with a Killing vector yields a conserved current, so integral energy conservation needs spacetime symmetry. [§19.5](r2r:chapter_19#195-能量动量张量)
  - [ ] Ricci tensor {kind: math} ^ricci-tensor
    - Contraction of the Riemann tensor measuring the initial volume acceleration of a cloud of freely falling particles. [§19.6](r2r:chapter_19#196-爱因斯坦场方程)
  - [ ] Einstein field equations {kind: physics} ^einstein-field-equations
    - R_ab - R g_ab/2 = -8 pi G T_ab, forced by the contracted Bianchi identity; the vacuum case means Ricci-flat. [§19.6](r2r:chapter_19#196-爱因斯坦场方程)
  - [ ] Cosmological constant {kind: physics} ^cosmological-constant
    - Term Lambda g_ab added by Einstein for a static universe, abandoned after Hubble, revived by distant-supernova observations. [§19.7](r2r:chapter_19#197-进一步的问题宇宙学常数外尔张量)
  - [ ] Weyl conformal tensor {kind: math} ^weyl-tensor
    - The Riemann tensor with Ricci part removed, describing gravity's free degrees of freedom; its vanishing means conformal flatness. [§19.7](r2r:chapter_19#197-进一步的问题宇宙学常数外尔张量)
  - [ ] Gravitational waves {kind: physics} ^gravitational-waves
    - Spacetime ripples carrying positive energy from orbiting bodies, confirmed indirectly by the Hulse-Taylor binary pulsar's orbital decay. [§19.8](r2r:chapter_19#198-引力场能量)
  - [ ] Where is gravitational field energy? {kind: question} ^q-gravitational-energy
    - Gravitational energy is real (it is carried off by waves) yet has no local density, by the equivalence principle. [§19.8](r2r:chapter_19#198-引力场能量)
  - [ ] Is there a cosmological constant? {kind: question} ^q-cosmological-constant
    - Einstein's discarded Lambda keeps returning; supernova data now suggest a small positive value or similar dark energy. [§19.7](r2r:chapter_19#197-进一步的问题宇宙学常数外尔张量)
  - [ ] Is the gauge potential physically real? {kind: question} ^q-reality-of-potential
    - A is not locally measurable, yet the Aharonov-Bohm effect shows its non-local loop integrals have observable effects. [§19.4](r2r:chapter_19#194-作为规范曲率的麦克斯韦场)

- [ ] 20. Lagrangians and Hamiltonians {kind: chapter} ^ch20
  - Recasts Newtonian mechanics in Lagrangian and Hamiltonian form, treats small oscillations and normal modes, links Hamiltonian flow to symplectic geometry, and extends the Lagrangian method to fields, Noether's theorem and modern theories. [Chapter 20](r2r:chapter_20)
  - [ ] Lagrangian formalism {kind: physics} ^lagrangian-formalism
    - Describes a system by a function L = K - V of generalized coordinates and independent generalized velocities on the tangent bundle of configuration space. [§20.1](r2r:chapter_20#201-神奇的拉格朗日形式体系)
  - [ ] Euler-Lagrange equations {kind: physics} ^euler-lagrange-equations
    - The equations of motion d/dt(dL/dq-dot) = dL/dq, recovering Newton's laws, e.g. Galileo's constant acceleration. [§20.1](r2r:chapter_20#201-神奇的拉格朗日形式体系)
  - [ ] Principle of stationary action {kind: physics} ^stationary-action
    - Hamilton's principle: actual motions make the action integral of L stationary among paths with fixed end points. [§20.1](r2r:chapter_20#201-神奇的拉格朗日形式体系)
  - [ ] Hamiltonian formalism {kind: physics} ^hamiltonian-formalism
    - Replaces velocities by generalized momenta, working in phase space (the cotangent bundle) with the total energy H as Hamiltonian. [§20.2](r2r:chapter_20#202-更为对称的哈密顿图像)
  - [ ] Hamilton's equations {kind: physics} ^hamilton-equations
    - The symmetric first-order equations dq/dt = dH/dp, dp/dt = -dH/dq, defining a flow on phase space. [§20.2](r2r:chapter_20#202-更为对称的哈密顿图像)
  - [ ] Simple harmonic motion {kind: physics} ^simple-harmonic-motion
    - Small oscillations obeying d^2q/dt^2 = -omega^2 q, sinusoidal with frequency omega/2pi, as for a small-amplitude pendulum. [§20.3](r2r:chapter_20#203-小振动)
  - [ ] Normal modes of vibration {kind: physics} ^normal-modes
    - Near stable equilibrium, quadratic Hamiltonians give oscillations decomposing into eigenvectors of W = PQ, each with its own frequency. [§20.3](r2r:chapter_20#203-小振动)
  - [ ] Hamiltonian flow and Poisson brackets {kind: math} ^hamiltonian-flow
    - Time evolution is the vector field {H, } on phase space; it preserves the symplectic form, and H itself is conserved. [§20.4](r2r:chapter_20#204-辛几何的哈密顿动力学)
  - [ ] Liouville's theorem {kind: physics} ^liouville-theorem
    - Hamiltonian evolution preserves phase-space volume, the N-th wedge power of the symplectic 2-form. [§20.4](r2r:chapter_20#204-辛几何的哈密顿动力学)
  - [ ] Functional derivative {kind: math} ^functional-derivative
    - Differentiation with respect to fields, giving Euler-Lagrange equations for infinite-dimensional configuration spaces. [§20.5](r2r:chapter_20#205-场的拉格朗日处理)
  - [ ] Lagrangian density for fields {kind: physics} ^field-lagrangian
    - A spacetime density whose 4-form integral is the action S; stationarity of S under field variations gives the field equations. [§20.5](r2r:chapter_20#205-场的拉格朗日处理)
  - [ ] Noether's theorem {kind: physics} ^noether-theorem
    - Each continuous symmetry of a Lagrangian yields a conserved quantity, e.g. gauge invariance gives conserved charge. [§20.6](r2r:chapter_20#206-如何从拉格朗日量导出现代理论)
  - [ ] Einstein-Hilbert action {kind: physics} ^einstein-hilbert-action
    - Hilbert's 1915 derivation of Einstein's equations from the scalar curvature R as Lagrangian density, alongside Maxwell's Lagrangian -F_ab F^ab/4. [§20.6](r2r:chapter_20#206-如何从拉格朗日量导出现代理论)
  - [ ] Is the Lagrangian the right way to find new theories? {kind: question} ^q-lagrangian-guidance
    - Nearly all new theories are posed as Lagrangians, but Penrose is uneasy that this gives little guidance on which physics is right. [§20.6](r2r:chapter_20#206-如何从拉格朗日量导出现代理论)
  - [ ] Can mathematical elegance mislead physics? {kind: question} ^q-formal-elegance
    - The perfect Hamiltonian picture of classical mechanics was overturned by quantum facts, yet still paved the way to quantum theory. [§20.4](r2r:chapter_20#204-辛几何的哈密顿动力学)

- [ ] 21. The quantum particle {kind: chapter} ^ch21
  - Builds the quantum mechanics of a single particle from non-commuting variables, the quantum Hamiltonian and Schrödinger's equation, reviews the experimental evidence for wave-particle duality, and examines wavefunctions, quantum jumps, probabilities, position and momentum states and the uncertainty relation. [Chapter 21](r2r:chapter_21)
  - [ ] Non-commuting variables {kind: math} ^non-commuting-variables
    - Heaviside's operator calculus treats d/dx algebraically, but D and x do not commute: Dx - xD = 1. [§21.1](r2r:chapter_21#211-非对易变量)
  - [ ] Canonical commutation relations {kind: physics} ^canonical-commutation-relations
    - Replacing momentum by i hbar d/dx gives [p_a, x^b] = i hbar delta_a^b between position and momentum. [§21.2](r2r:chapter_21#212-量子哈密顿量)
  - [ ] Canonical quantization {kind: physics} ^canonical-quantization
    - Substituting momentum operators into the classical Hamiltonian gives a quantum Hamiltonian, subject to factor-ordering ambiguities. [§21.2](r2r:chapter_21#212-量子哈密顿量)
  - [ ] Wavefunction {kind: physics} ^wavefunction
    - A complex function psi of position (not momentum) giving the quantum state of the system. [§21.3](r2r:chapter_21#213-薛定谔方程)
  - [ ] Schrödinger equation {kind: physics} ^schrodinger-equation
    - i hbar dpsi/dt = H psi, the deterministic time evolution of the wavefunction under the quantum Hamiltonian. [§21.3](r2r:chapter_21#213-薛定谔方程)
  - [ ] Planck's constant and E = h nu {kind: physics} ^planck-constant
    - Planck's 1900 black-body analysis, resolving the Rayleigh-Jeans failure, introduced energy quanta E = h nu; hbar = h/2pi. [§21.4](r2r:chapter_21#214-量子理论的实验背景)
  - [ ] de Broglie wavelength {kind: physics} ^de-broglie-wavelength
    - A particle with momentum p has wavelength h/p, seen in Davisson-Germer electron diffraction from crystals. [§21.4](r2r:chapter_21#214-量子理论的实验背景)
  - [ ] Double-slit experiment {kind: physics} ^double-slit-experiment
    - Single particles arrive as localized spots yet build an interference pattern whose fringe spacing gives the de Broglie wavelength. [§21.4](r2r:chapter_21#214-量子理论的实验背景)
  - [ ] Wave-particle duality {kind: physics} ^wave-particle-duality
    - Particles behave as waves and waves as particles; momentum eigenfunctions (plane waves) reconcile operator momentum with classical momentum. [§21.5](r2r:chapter_21#215-理解波粒二象性)
  - [ ] Eigenstates of observables {kind: physics} ^eigenstates
    - States on which an observable operator acts as multiplication by a number, its eigenvalue, which is the definite measured value. [§21.5](r2r:chapter_21#215-理解波粒二象性)
  - [ ] Momentum states {kind: physics} ^momentum-states
    - Plane waves exp(-iP.x/hbar), eigenstates of momentum with definite 4-momentum, completely spread out in space. [§21.5](r2r:chapter_21#215-理解波粒二象性)
  - [ ] Wave packet {kind: physics} ^wave-packet
    - A localized wavefunction built from momentum states, e.g. a Gaussian times a plane wave, approximating a classical particle. [§21.6](r2r:chapter_21#216-什么是量子实在)
  - [ ] Mach-Zehnder interferometer {kind: physics} ^mach-zehnder-interferometer
    - Beam-splitter experiments where one photon shows particle or wave behaviour by setup, revealing the holistic, non-local wavefunction. [§21.7](r2r:chapter_21#217-波函数的整体性质)
  - [ ] Quantum jump (state reduction) {kind: physics} ^wavefunction-collapse
    - On measurement the state suddenly collapses to a localized form, a process not described by Schrödinger evolution. [§21.8](r2r:chapter_21#218-奇怪的量子跳变)
  - [ ] Born probability rule {kind: physics} ^born-rule
    - |psi|^2 gives the probability density for position of a normalized wavefunction; states are rays, psi ~ k psi. [§21.9](r2r:chapter_21#219-波函数的概率分布)
  - [ ] Normalized wavefunction {kind: physics} ^normalized-wavefunction
    - A wavefunction whose |psi|^2 integrates to 1; momentum and position states are not normalizable idealizations. [§21.9](r2r:chapter_21#219-波函数的概率分布)
  - [ ] Position states {kind: physics} ^position-states
    - Dirac delta functions delta(x - X), eigenstates of position, the infinitely concentrated opposite of momentum states. [§21.10](r2r:chapter_21#2110-位置态)
  - [ ] Momentum-space representation {kind: physics} ^momentum-space
    - The Fourier transform of psi(x) represents the same state as a function of momentum, swapping roles of position and momentum states. [§21.11](r2r:chapter_21#2111-动量空间描述)
  - [ ] Heisenberg uncertainty relation {kind: physics} ^uncertainty-principle
    - Spreads satisfy Delta x Delta p >= hbar/2, reflecting incompatible non-commuting measurements; a similar energy-time relation limits unstable lifetimes. [§21.11](r2r:chapter_21#2111-动量空间描述)
  - [ ] What is quantum reality? {kind: question} ^q-quantum-reality
    - Penrose argues the wavefunction (state vector), not the probabilities alone, is what is physically real. [§21.6](r2r:chapter_21#216-什么是量子实在)
  - [ ] How can a particle also be a wave? {kind: question} ^q-wave-particle
    - Double-slit and beam-splitter results defeat purely wave or purely particle pictures, demanding the holistic wavefunction. [§21.5](r2r:chapter_21#215-理解波粒二象性)
  - [ ] Measurement problem {kind: question} ^q-measurement-problem
    - What physical circumstances make a measurement, and why the continuous Schrödinger evolution gives way to a discontinuous jump. [§21.8](r2r:chapter_21#218-奇怪的量子跳变)
  - [ ] Is the wavefunction just a probability wave? {kind: question} ^q-probability-wave
    - Complex amplitudes and destructive interference show psi cannot be merely a probability, so it must be more real. [§21.9](r2r:chapter_21#219-波函数的概率分布)

- [ ] 22. Quantum algebra, geometry, and spin {kind: chapter} ^ch22
  - Lays out the formal machinery of quantum mechanics (Hilbert space, unitary evolution, observables, projective measurement) and the geometry of spin, from the Riemann sphere of two-state systems to the Majorana description and relativistic angular momentum. [Chapter 22](r2r:chapter_22)
  - [ ] Unitary evolution (U) {kind: physics} ^unitary-evolution
    - Deterministic, linear Schrodinger evolution of the state vector, one of the two radically different quantum procedures, preserving scalar products. [§22.1](r2r:chapter_22#221-量子步骤-u-和-r)
  - [ ] Linearity of U {kind: physics} ^quantum-linearity
    - Schrodinger evolution preserves superpositions; applied to detectors it yields superposed outcomes, the root conflict with R. [§22.2](r2r:chapter_22#222-u-的线性性以及它给-r-带来的问题)
  - [ ] Hilbert space {kind: math} ^hilbert-space
    - Complex vector space of normalizable states with a positive-definite Hermitian scalar product, the arena of quantum states. [§22.3](r2r:chapter_22#223-幺正结构希尔伯特空间和狄拉克算符)
  - [ ] Dirac bra-ket notation {kind: math} ^dirac-notation
    - Kets |psi> in H and dual bras <phi| whose bracket gives the Hermitian scalar product; operator matrix elements <phi|L|psi>. [§22.3](r2r:chapter_22#223-幺正结构希尔伯特空间和狄拉克算符)
  - [ ] Schrodinger and Heisenberg pictures {kind: physics} ^heisenberg-picture
    - Equivalent descriptions moving time dependence from states to operators; yields the Heisenberg equation of motion i hbar dQ/dt = [H,Q]. [§22.4](r2r:chapter_22#224-幺正演化薛定谔绘景和海森伯绘景)
  - [ ] Quantum observables {kind: physics} ^quantum-observables
    - Measurable quantities as linear operators; Penrose argues normal (not necessarily Hermitian) operators with orthogonal eigenvectors suffice. [§22.5](r2r:chapter_22#225-量子可观察量)
  - [ ] Yes/no measurements and projectors {kind: physics} ^projection-postulate
    - Projectors E with E = E* = E^2 represent yes/no questions; the projection postulate fixes the post-measurement state even for degenerate eigenvalues. [§22.6](r2r:chapter_22#226-yesno-测量投影算符)
  - [ ] Null (interaction-free) measurement {kind: physics} ^null-measurement
    - Non-detection still reduces the state; illustrated by the Elitzur-Vaidman bomb test in a Mach-Zehnder interferometer. [§22.7](r2r:chapter_22#227-类光测量螺旋性)
  - [ ] Helicity {kind: physics} ^helicity
    - Spin of a massless particle about its direction of motion, s = +-j; photon helicity states are circular polarizations. [§22.7](r2r:chapter_22#227-类光测量螺旋性)
  - [ ] Quantum spin, Pauli matrices and angular momentum commutators {kind: physics} ^spin
    - Spin as a representation of the SO(3) Lie algebra, [L1,L2]=i hbar L3; spin 1/2 uses two-component wavefunctions and Pauli matrices. [§22.8](r2r:chapter_22#228-自旋和旋量)
  - [ ] Two-spinor formalism {kind: math} ^two-spinors
    - Spin-n/2 states as totally symmetric spinors psi_AB...F with abstract indices, a calculus that can supplement or replace tensors. [§22.8](r2r:chapter_22#228-自旋和旋量)
  - [ ] Casimir operators {kind: math} ^casimir-operators
    - Operators commuting with all group generators (J^2 for SO(3); p_a p^a and S_a S^a for Poincare), labelling irreducible representations. [§22.12](r2r:chapter_22#2212-相对论性量子角动量)
  - [ ] Riemann sphere of two-state systems {kind: physics} ^bloch-sphere
    - Spin-1/2 (or polarization) states form PH^2, a Riemann sphere identified with spatial spin directions; orthogonal states are antipodal. [§22.9](r2r:chapter_22#229-二态系统的黎曼球面)
  - [ ] Majorana description of higher spin {kind: physics} ^majorana-description
    - A spin-n/2 state corresponds to n unordered points on the sphere, interpreted via null outcomes of Stern-Gerlach measurements. [§22.10](r2r:chapter_22#2210-高自旋马约拉纳绘景)
  - [ ] Spherical harmonics {kind: math} ^spherical-harmonics
    - Eigenfunctions of the Laplacian on S^2 with eigenvalues -j(j+1) and d/dphi eigenvalue im, linked to spin-weighted harmonics and Majorana points. [§22.11](r2r:chapter_22#2211-球谐函数)
  - [ ] Pauli-Lubanski spin vector {kind: physics} ^pauli-lubanski-vector
    - Relativistic spin S^a built from p_a and M^ab; with p_a p^a it gives the two Poincare Casimirs, reducing to helicity when massless. [§22.12](r2r:chapter_22#2212-相对论性量子角动量)
  - [ ] Quantum harmonic oscillator {kind: physics} ^quantum-harmonic-oscillator
    - Each normal mode has energies (n + 1/2) hbar omega, used to approximate internal degrees of freedom of isolated quantum objects. [§22.13](r2r:chapter_22#2213-一般的孤立量子客体)
  - [ ] Bohr atom and hydrogen spectrum {kind: physics} ^bohr-atom
    - Quantized Kepler orbits and hydrogen stationary states explaining spectral lines (Balmer formula); discreteness traced to compactness. [§22.13](r2r:chapter_22#2213-一般的孤立量子客体)
  - [ ] How can U and R coexist? {kind: question} ^q-u-and-r-consistency
    - Linear Schrodinger evolution would superpose detector responses, contradicting single outcomes; many-worlds is mentioned, and a deeper theory is anticipated. [§22.2](r2r:chapter_22#222-u-的线性性以及它给-r-带来的问题)
  - [ ] Does the state vector describe reality? {kind: question} ^q-state-vector-reality
    - Most physicists treat collapse as unphysical; Penrose argues the Heisenberg picture obscures quantum reality while the Schrodinger picture keeps it visible. [§22.4](r2r:chapter_22#224-幺正演化薛定谔绘景和海森伯绘景)
  - [ ] Why does large quantum spin look so unclassical? {kind: question} ^q-quantum-vs-classical-spin
    - A generic large-j state has Majorana points scattered randomly over the sphere, unlike a classical spin clustered about one axis. [§22.11](r2r:chapter_22#2211-球谐函数)

- [ ] 23. The entangled quantum world {kind: chapter} ^ch23
  - Treats many-particle quantum states, showing how entanglement arises from tensor products, how EPR, Bell and Hardy examples defeat local realism, how bosons and fermions are distinguished, and how teleportation illustrates 'quanglement'. [Chapter 23](r2r:chapter_23)
  - [ ] Many-particle wavefunction {kind: physics} ^many-particle-wavefunction
    - One wavefunction on the joint configuration space of all particles with a single shared time, not one wavefunction per particle. [§23.1](r2r:chapter_23#231-多粒子系统的量子力学)
  - [ ] Vastness of many-particle state space {kind: physics} ^many-particle-state-space
    - Freedom grows as infinity^(2 infinity^(3n)) rather than n separate fields; a ten-point toy universe makes the exponential growth concrete. [§23.2](r2r:chapter_23#232-巨大的多粒子系统态空间)
  - [ ] Tensor product of Hilbert spaces {kind: math} ^tensor-product-states
    - Composite states live in H^p tensor H^q; only a tiny subset are simple products |psi>|phi>, the rest being entangled. [§23.3](r2r:chapter_23#233-量子纠缠贝尔不等式)
  - [ ] Quantum entanglement {kind: physics} ^entanglement
    - States of a composite system that cannot be written as products, so parts lack individual state vectors; Schrodinger's 1935 notion. [§23.3](r2r:chapter_23#233-量子纠缠贝尔不等式)
  - [ ] EPR effects {kind: physics} ^epr-effects
    - Correlations between distant measurements on entangled parts that no local, classical-like account can reproduce. [§23.3](r2r:chapter_23#233-量子纠缠贝尔不等式)
  - [ ] Bell inequalities {kind: physics} ^bell-inequalities
    - Bounds on joint probabilities of separated measurements obeyed by any local hidden-variable model, violated by quantum predictions. [§23.3](r2r:chapter_23#233-量子纠缠贝尔不等式)
  - [ ] Bohm-type EPR experiment {kind: physics} ^epr-bohm
    - Two spin-1/2 particles in a spin-0 singlet; Stapp's arrangement of measurement directions shows a Bell-inequality violation. [§23.4](r2r:chapter_23#234-玻姆型-epr-实验)
  - [ ] Hardy's EPR example {kind: physics} ^hardy-example
    - An almost probability-free nonlocality argument using yes/no outcomes with certainty, the remaining event occurring 1/12 of the time. [§23.5](r2r:chapter_23#235-哈迪的-epr-事例几乎与概率无关)
  - [ ] Bosons and fermions {kind: physics} ^bosons-and-fermions
    - Identical particles have wavefunctions symmetric (bosons) or antisymmetric (fermions) under exchange, changing how states are counted. [§23.7](r2r:chapter_23#237-玻色子和费米子)
  - [ ] Pauli exclusion principle {kind: physics} ^pauli-exclusion-principle
    - Antisymmetry forbids two identical fermions occupying the same state, which stabilizes ordinary matter and structures chemistry. [§23.7](r2r:chapter_23#237-玻色子和费米子)
  - [ ] Bose-Einstein condensation {kind: physics} ^bose-einstein-condensation
    - At low temperatures bosons crowd into one state, underlying superfluidity, lasers and (via Cooper pairs) superconductivity. [§23.7](r2r:chapter_23#237-玻色子和费米子)
  - [ ] Symmetrized and antisymmetrized states {kind: math} ^symmetrized-states
    - Identical boson states psi^(alpha phi^beta...) and fermion states psi^[alpha phi^beta...] in abstract-index form, the least-entangled options available. [§23.8](r2r:chapter_23#238-玻色子和费米子的量子态)
  - [ ] Quantum teleportation {kind: physics} ^quantum-teleportation
    - Transferring an unknown spin state using a shared EPR pair, a Bell-basis measurement and two classical bits, then a corrective rotation. [§23.9](r2r:chapter_23#239-量子隐形传态)
  - [ ] Quanglement {kind: physics} ^quanglement
    - Penrose's term for quantum information: a non-signalling influence that can be pictured as running backward in time along EPR channels. [§23.10](r2r:chapter_23#2310-量子纠缠)
  - [ ] No faster-than-light signalling {kind: physics} ^no-signalling
    - EPR correlations cannot transmit classical information acausally, so they are compatible with relativity despite seeming nonlocal. [§23.10](r2r:chapter_23#2310-量子纠缠)
  - [ ] How should we understand entanglement? {kind: question} ^q-two-mysteries-of-entanglement
    - The first mystery: how to grasp entanglement conceptually, given that no local realistic model reproduces Bell-violating correlations. [§23.6](r2r:chapter_23#236-量子纠缠的两个谜团)
  - [ ] Why don't we notice entanglement? {kind: question} ^q-why-entanglement-unnoticed
    - Almost all states are entangled and U only increases entanglement; Penrose argues R must be what keeps the everyday world disentangled. [§23.6](r2r:chapter_23#236-量子纠缠的两个谜团)
  - [ ] How do EPR effects square with relativity? {kind: question} ^q-epr-and-relativity
    - Measurements on spacelike-separated parts have no invariant order, so which one 'causes' reduction is undefined; quanglement offers a partial picture. [§23.10](r2r:chapter_23#2310-量子纠缠)

- [ ] 24. Dirac's electron and antiparticles {kind: chapter} ^ch24
  - Shows how combining quantum theory with special relativity forces antiparticles and quantum fields, and how Dirac's Clifford-algebra square root of the wave operator yields the electron equation, its spin and magnetic moment, and the positron. [Chapter 24](r2r:chapter_24)
  - [ ] Quantum field theory (motivation) {kind: physics} ^quantum-field-theory
    - Relativity plus quantum theory does not conserve particle number, so the basic entity becomes a quantum field whose excitations are particles. [§24.2](r2r:chapter_24#242-为什么反粒子意味着量子场)
  - [ ] Pair creation and annihilation {kind: physics} ^pair-creation
    - Enough concentrated energy (at least twice the rest energy) produces particle-antiparticle pairs, so single-particle quantum mechanics breaks down. [§24.2](r2r:chapter_24#242-为什么反粒子意味着量子场)
  - [ ] Positive frequency and energy positivity {kind: physics} ^positive-frequency
    - Physical states must have positive energy, i.e. wavefunctions with only positive-frequency time dependence, tied to Riemann-sphere frequency splitting. [§24.3](r2r:chapter_24#243-量子力学里能量的正定性)
  - [ ] Relativistic square-root Hamiltonian {kind: physics} ^relativistic-hamiltonian
    - The relativistic energy requires the square root of a differential operator, with a hidden sign ambiguity admitting negative energies. [§24.4](r2r:chapter_24#244-相对论能量公式的困难)
  - [ ] Wave operator (d'Alembertian) {kind: math} ^wave-operator
    - The Lorentz-invariant second-order operator box = d^2/dt^2 - grad^2, replacing the non-invariant d/dt of Schrodinger's equation. [§24.5](r2r:chapter_24#245-partialpartial-t-的非不变性)
  - [ ] Klein-Gordon equation {kind: physics} ^klein-gordon-equation
    - (box + M^2) psi = 0 with M = mu/hbar, the relativistic wave equation for massive spinless particles, second order in time. [§24.5](r2r:chapter_24#245-partialpartial-t-的非不变性)
  - [ ] Dirac operator (Clifford-Dirac square root) {kind: math} ^dirac-operator
    - Using Lorentzian Clifford elements gamma_a, the first-order operator d-slash = gamma^a d_a squares to the wave operator. [§24.6](r2r:chapter_24#246-波算符的克利福德狄拉克平方根)
  - [ ] Dirac equation {kind: physics} ^dirac-equation
    - (d-slash + iM) psi = 0, first order in time yet Lorentz invariant; its spinor wavefunction automatically describes electron spin. [§24.7](r2r:chapter_24#247-狄拉克方程)
  - [ ] Electron magnetic moment {kind: physics} ^dirac-magnetic-moment
    - Coupled to an electromagnetic potential, the Dirac electron acts as a magnet of definite strength, matching experiment up to QED corrections. [§24.7](r2r:chapter_24#247-狄拉克方程)
  - [ ] Four-component Dirac spinor {kind: math} ^dirac-spinor
    - Gamma matrices need 4x4 representation, so psi has four components versus Pauli's two, yet describes a two-state spin-1/2 particle. [§24.8](r2r:chapter_24#248-正电子的狄拉克途径)
  - [ ] Dirac sea {kind: physics} ^dirac-sea
    - Negative-energy states are all filled, so the Pauli principle blocks electrons falling in; a hole behaves as a positive antielectron. [§24.8](r2r:chapter_24#248-正电子的狄拉克途径)
  - [ ] Positron {kind: physics} ^positron
    - The electron's antiparticle predicted by Dirac's hole theory (first mistaken for the proton) and discovered by Anderson in 1932. [§24.8](r2r:chapter_24#248-正电子的狄拉克途径)
  - [ ] Can quantum theory be reconciled with relativity? {kind: question} ^q-qm-relativity-tension
    - The fusion forces quantum fields yet QFT remains mathematically not fully consistent, needing tricks; the tension persists. [§24.1](r2r:chapter_24#241-量子理论与相对论之间的张力)
  - [ ] What to do with negative-energy solutions? {kind: question} ^q-negative-energy
    - Relativistic equations admit negative frequencies that would make matter unstable; Dirac's sea turns the problem into a prediction of antimatter. [§24.4](r2r:chapter_24#244-相对论能量公式的困难)

- [ ] 25. The standard model of particle physics {kind: chapter} ^ch25
  - A qualitative tour of the standard model through Penrose's zigzag picture of the Dirac electron: weak-interaction chirality, C, P, T and CPT, the electroweak U(2) symmetry and Higgs field, hadrons, quarks and colour SU(3), and prospects beyond. [Chapter 25](r2r:chapter_25)
  - [ ] Standard model of particle physics {kind: physics} ^standard-model
    - The current framework describing all known particles and the strong, weak and electromagnetic forces, grown from the particle zoo after 1928. [§25.1](r2r:chapter_25#251-现代粒子物理学的起源)
  - [ ] Zigzag picture of the electron {kind: physics} ^zigzag-picture
    - In two-spinor form the Dirac electron is a massless left-handed zig and right-handed zag continually converting, coupled by mass. [§25.2](r2r:chapter_25#252-电子的-zigzag-图像)
  - [ ] Chirality and gamma-5 projections {kind: physics} ^chirality
    - Left- and right-handed parts (1 -+ gamma_5) psi correspond to the unprimed and primed two-spinors of the Dirac field. [§25.2](r2r:chapter_25#252-电子的-zigzag-图像)
  - [ ] Feynman diagrams {kind: physics} ^feynman-diagrams
    - Spacetime graphs of particle processes (emission, absorption, exchange); quantum-mechanically, all such processes are superposed. [§25.2](r2r:chapter_25#252-电子的-zigzag-图像)
  - [ ] Weak interaction and W, Z bosons {kind: physics} ^weak-interaction
    - Force behind beta decay, once Fermi's point interaction, now mediated by massive vector bosons W+-, Z0 coupling only to zig parts. [§25.3](r2r:chapter_25#253-电弱相互作用反射不对称性)
  - [ ] Parity violation {kind: physics} ^parity-violation
    - Wu's cobalt-60 experiment showed weak processes are mirror-asymmetric because only left-handed zigs participate. [§25.3](r2r:chapter_25#253-电弱相互作用反射不对称性)
  - [ ] Weyl equation (massless neutrino) {kind: physics} ^weyl-equation
    - Setting M = 0 decouples the two-spinor Dirac equations into separate Weyl equations; only the zig type couples weakly as a neutrino. [§25.3](r2r:chapter_25#253-电弱相互作用反射不对称性)
  - [ ] C, P, T and the CPT theorem {kind: physics} ^cpt-theorem
    - Charge conjugation, parity and time reversal are individually (and CP) violated, but CPT is exact; CP violation seen in K0 decays. [§25.4](r2r:chapter_25#254-正反共轭宇称和时间反演)
  - [ ] Antiparticles as particles going backward in time {kind: physics} ^antiparticle-time-reversal
    - Via CPT, C equals PT, so Feynman-Stueckelberg treat antiparticles as time-reversed particles, giving crossing symmetry in diagrams. [§25.4](r2r:chapter_25#254-正反共轭宇称和时间反演)
  - [ ] Electroweak theory and U(2) symmetry {kind: physics} ^electroweak-theory
    - Weak and electromagnetic forces unified under SU(2)xU(1) (really U(2)) rotating W+-, Z0, gamma; predicted W and Z masses confirmed at CERN 1983. [§25.5](r2r:chapter_25#255-电弱对称群)
  - [ ] Higgs field and symmetry breaking {kind: physics} ^higgs-field
    - Masses break U(2) symmetry; the zig-zag coupling constant is reinterpreted as a Higgs field selecting the observed mass eigenstates. [§25.5](r2r:chapter_25#255-电弱对称群)
  - [ ] Weinberg (weak mixing) angle {kind: physics} ^weinberg-angle
    - Parameter describing the mixing of Z0 and photon within electroweak theory, one of the standard model's unexplained constants. [§25.7](r2r:chapter_25#257-色夸克)
  - [ ] Hadrons, baryons, mesons and leptons {kind: physics} ^hadrons
    - Strongly interacting particles split into fermionic baryons and bosonic mesons; leptons (electron, muon, tau, neutrinos) feel no strong force. [§25.6](r2r:chapter_25#256-强相互作用粒子)
  - [ ] Quark model and the eightfold way {kind: physics} ^quark-model
    - Hadron multiplets as SU(3)/Z3 representations, explained by fractionally charged quarks u, d, s; predicted the Omega-minus. [§25.6](r2r:chapter_25#256-强相互作用粒子)
  - [ ] Colour quarks {kind: physics} ^colour-charge
    - Each quark flavour comes in three unobservable colours, curing the spin-statistics problem and supplying an exact SU(3) symmetry. [§25.7](r2r:chapter_25#257-色夸克)
  - [ ] Quantum chromodynamics (QCD) {kind: physics} ^quantum-chromodynamics
    - Non-Abelian gauge theory of colour SU(3) whose self-interacting gluons bind quarks, with three generations of quark doublets. [§25.7](r2r:chapter_25#257-色夸克)
  - [ ] Cabibbo angle and flavour mixing {kind: physics} ^cabibbo-angle
    - Strong-interaction and weak-interaction eigenstates of quarks are rotated relative to each other, as with K0 into K_L and K_S. [§25.7](r2r:chapter_25#257-色夸克)
  - [ ] Grand unified theories (GUTs) {kind: physics} ^grand-unified-theories
    - Proposed larger symmetry groups containing SU(3) and U(2) to unify strong and electroweak forces; none yet accepted. [§25.8](r2r:chapter_25#258-超越标准模型)
  - [ ] Are the zig and zag particles real? {kind: question} ^q-are-zigzags-real
    - Penrose argues the massless zig and zag are as real as the Dirac electron itself, raising how far mathematical descriptions capture reality. [§25.2](r2r:chapter_25#252-电子的-zigzag-图像)
  - [ ] How can a badly broken SU(2) be a gauge group? {kind: question} ^q-broken-gauge-symmetry
    - Penrose doubts that a heavily broken symmetry can serve as a genuine gauge symmetry, contrasting exact colour SU(3). [§25.8](r2r:chapter_25#258-超越标准模型)
  - [ ] What lies beyond the standard model? {kind: question} ^q-beyond-standard-model
    - Some 17 unexplained parameters and the odd SU(3)/U(2) asymmetry motivate GUTs and the Chan-Tsou dual-group proposal. [§25.8](r2r:chapter_25#258-超越标准模型)

- [ ] 26. Quantum field theory {kind: chapter} ^ch26
  - Builds quantum field theory from creation and annihilation operators, path integrals and Feynman diagrams, and confronts its infinities through renormalization and the choice of vacuum. [Chapter 26](r2r:chapter_26)
  - [ ] Creation and annihilation operators {kind: physics} ^creation-annihilation-operators
    - Second quantization: wavefunction operators add or remove particles from the vacuum, commuting for bosons and anticommuting for fermions. [§26.2](r2r:chapter_26#262-产生算符和湮没算符)
  - [ ] Vacuum state {kind: physics} ^vacuum-state
    - The state |0> with no particles, annihilated by every annihilation operator, on which creation operators build multi-particle states. [§26.2](r2r:chapter_26#262-产生算符和湮没算符)
  - [ ] Infinite-dimensional algebra of field operators {kind: math} ^field-operator-algebra
    - Fermion field operators satisfy an infinite-dimensional Clifford algebra, with infinite-dimensional Hermitian, symplectic and orthogonal forms. [§26.3](r2r:chapter_26#263-无穷维代数)
  - [ ] Complex structure on field space {kind: math} ^field-complex-structure
    - An operator J with J^2 = -1 acts as i on positive-frequency and -i on negative-frequency free fields, turning classical field space complex. [§26.3](r2r:chapter_26#263-无穷维代数)
  - [ ] Majorana spinor field {kind: physics} ^majorana-spinors
    - A spinor field whose particle is its own antiparticle, a possibility for massive neutrinos; contrasted with Dirac particles. [§26.4](r2r:chapter_26#264-量子场论中的反粒子)
  - [ ] Inequivalent (alternative) vacua {kind: physics} ^inequivalent-vacua
    - Vacua such as |0> and the Dirac sea that no operator of the field algebra connects, so they define distinct quantum theories. [§26.5](r2r:chapter_26#265-备择真空)
  - [ ] Feynman path integral (sum over histories) {kind: physics} ^path-integral
    - Each classical history gets amplitude exp(iS/hbar) from its action; summing all histories recovers classical paths plus quantum corrections. [§26.6](r2r:chapter_26#266-相互作用拉格朗日量和路径积分)
  - [ ] Feynman propagator {kind: physics} ^feynman-propagator
    - The formally summed amplitude for a free particle between two points, with an i-epsilon prescription fixing positive frequency; a line in a Feynman diagram. [§26.7](r2r:chapter_26#267-发散的路径积分费恩曼响应)
  - [ ] Mass shell {kind: physics} ^mass-shell
    - The hyperboloid P_a P^a = M^2 in momentum space; real external particles lie on it, virtual internal lines may be off-shell. [§26.8](r2r:chapter_26#268-构建费恩曼图s-矩阵)
  - [ ] S-matrix (scattering matrix) {kind: physics} ^s-matrix
    - The infinite matrix of amplitudes from in-states to out-states, computed by summing Feynman diagrams; the main goal of QFT. [§26.8](r2r:chapter_26#268-构建费恩曼图s-矩阵)
  - [ ] Tree and loop diagrams {kind: physics} ^tree-and-loop-diagrams
    - Tree diagrams fix internal momenta and reproduce classical theory; closed loops leave free momenta whose integrals diverge. [§26.8](r2r:chapter_26#268-构建费恩曼图s-矩阵)
  - [ ] Renormalization {kind: physics} ^renormalization
    - Absorbing divergent diagram contributions into a finite number of rescaled parameters such as charge and mass; renormalizable theories allow this. [§26.9](r2r:chapter_26#269-重正化)
  - [ ] Ultraviolet divergence {kind: physics} ^ultraviolet-divergence
    - Infinities in loop integrals arising from arbitrarily large momenta, i.e. arbitrarily small distances; the most serious QFT divergences. [§26.9](r2r:chapter_26#269-重正化)
  - [ ] Vacuum polarization {kind: physics} ^vacuum-polarization
    - Virtual electron-positron pairs screen an electron's bare charge, the physical basis of charge renormalization. [§26.9](r2r:chapter_26#269-重正化)
  - [ ] Asymptotic freedom {kind: physics} ^asymptotic-freedom
    - The strong force weakens at very high momenta, making QCD calculable despite its large coupling constant. [§26.9](r2r:chapter_26#269-重正化)
  - [ ] Fine-structure constant {kind: physics} ^fine-structure-constant
    - The electromagnetic coupling constant, about 1/137, compared with the strong coupling and the even weaker weak force. [§26.9](r2r:chapter_26#269-重正化)
  - [ ] Perturbation expansion {kind: math} ^perturbation-expansion
    - Feynman diagrams are terms of a power series in the coupling constant; even renormalized QED's series probably does not converge. [§26.10](r2r:chapter_26#2610-拉格朗日量的费恩曼图)
  - [ ] Are the infinities of QFT a sign of a deeper flaw? {kind: question} ^q-qft-infinities
    - Whether renormalization merely hides divergences that a future theory, perhaps involving gravity, must genuinely remove. [§26.9](r2r:chapter_26#269-重正化)
  - [ ] Do divergent path integrals have mathematical meaning? {kind: question} ^q-path-integral-meaning
    - The path integral lacks a proper infinite-dimensional measure; physicists trust a Euler-like 'Platonic' sense of divergent sums. [§26.7](r2r:chapter_26#267-发散的路径积分费恩曼响应)
  - [ ] Which vacuum does nature choose? {kind: question} ^q-choice-of-vacuum
    - Symmetry breaking in electroweak theory is modelled by choosing an inequivalent vacuum, raising the question of its physical status. [§26.11](r2r:chapter_26#2611-费恩曼图和真空选择)

- [ ] 27. The Big Bang and its thermodynamic legacy {kind: chapter} ^ch27
  - Explains entropy and the second law, traces the arrow of time to an extraordinarily special low-entropy Big Bang, and introduces black holes, their entropy and standard cosmological models. [Chapter 27](r2r:chapter_27)
  - [ ] Time symmetry of dynamical laws {kind: physics} ^time-reversal-symmetry
    - Classical and quantum (U) dynamical equations are symmetric under time reversal, so initial and final data are equally good determinants. [§27.1](r2r:chapter_27#271-动力学演化的时间对称性)
  - [ ] Chaotic systems {kind: physics} ^chaotic-systems
    - Deterministic systems whose behaviour depends extremely sensitively on initial data, like a magnetic pendulum toy or the weather. [§27.2](r2r:chapter_27#272-亚微观成分)
  - [ ] Second law of thermodynamics {kind: physics} ^second-law
    - Heat flows from hotter to colder bodies; more precisely, entropy of an isolated system increases, making retrodiction rather than prediction hard. [§27.2](r2r:chapter_27#272-亚微观成分)
  - [ ] Entropy {kind: physics} ^entropy
    - Measure of disorder introduced by Clausius; the quantity that the second law says does not decrease. [§27.3](r2r:chapter_27#273-熵)
  - [ ] Coarse graining {kind: physics} ^coarse-graining
    - Partition of phase space into boxes of macroscopically indistinguishable states, on which Boltzmann's entropy is defined. [§27.3](r2r:chapter_27#273-熵)
  - [ ] Boltzmann entropy formula {kind: physics} ^boltzmann-entropy
    - S = k log V, with V the phase-space volume of the coarse-graining box; the logarithm makes entropy additive and robust. [§27.3](r2r:chapter_27#273-熵)
  - [ ] Thermal equilibrium {kind: physics} ^thermal-equilibrium
    - The maximal-entropy box, whose volume nearly fills phase space; for a gas it gives the Maxwell velocity distribution and equipartition. [§27.4](r2r:chapter_27#274-熵概念的鲁棒性)
  - [ ] Gravitational clumping and entropy {kind: physics} ^gravitational-entropy
    - With gravity, uniform matter is low-entropy and clumping raises entropy, explaining why the Sun is a usable low-entropy source. [§27.7](r2r:chapter_27#277-大爆炸的角色)
  - [ ] Big Bang {kind: physics} ^big-bang
    - Singular origin of the universe, supported by Friedmann's solutions, Hubble's expansion and the background radiation; the region B of phase space. [§27.7](r2r:chapter_27#277-大爆炸的角色)
  - [ ] Cosmic microwave background {kind: physics} ^cosmic-microwave-background
    - The 2.7 K radiation filling space, a redshifted relic of the Big Bang with an almost exact Planck black-body spectrum. [§27.7](r2r:chapter_27#277-大爆炸的角色)
  - [ ] Black holes {kind: physics} ^black-holes
    - Regions formed by gravitational collapse, first modelled by the Schwarzschild solution, from which not even light escapes. [§27.8](r2r:chapter_27#278-黑-洞)
  - [ ] Chandrasekhar limit {kind: physics} ^chandrasekhar-limit
    - Mass above which electron degeneracy pressure, from the Pauli principle, cannot support a cold star against collapse. [§27.8](r2r:chapter_27#278-黑-洞)
  - [ ] Event horizon {kind: physics} ^event-horizon
    - Null hypersurface separating events that can signal to infinity from those trapped in the black hole; locally unremarkable to infallers. [§27.9](r2r:chapter_27#279-事件视界与时空奇点)
  - [ ] Spacetime singularities {kind: physics} ^spacetime-singularities
    - Under Einstein's equations with positive energy and mild assumptions, collapse inevitably produces singularities of infinite curvature. [§27.9](r2r:chapter_27#279-事件视界与时空奇点)
  - [ ] Cosmic censorship {kind: physics} ^cosmic-censorship
    - Unproved conjecture that singularities from gravitational collapse are always hidden inside event horizons. [§27.9](r2r:chapter_27#279-事件视界与时空奇点)
  - [ ] Planck units {kind: physics} ^planck-units
    - Units with c = G = hbar = k = 1; the Planck length is about 1.6e-35 m and Planck time 5.3e-44 s. [§27.10](r2r:chapter_27#2710-黑洞熵)
  - [ ] Bekenstein-Hawking black hole entropy {kind: physics} ^bekenstein-hawking-entropy
    - Black-hole entropy is a quarter of horizon area in Planck units; final Kerr holes need ten parameters yet carry enormous entropy. [§27.10](r2r:chapter_27#2710-黑洞熵)
  - [ ] Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker models {kind: physics} ^flrw-models
    - Spatially homogeneous isotropic cosmologies with K > 0, 0 or < 0, expanding from a Big Bang, with or without cosmological constant. [§27.11](r2r:chapter_27#2711-宇宙学)
  - [ ] Conformal (Penrose) diagrams {kind: math} ^conformal-diagrams
    - Diagrams drawing spacetime with null lines at 45 degrees and infinity as a finite boundary, displaying causal structure. [§27.12](r2r:chapter_27#2712-共形图)
  - [ ] Particle horizon {kind: physics} ^particle-horizon
    - Because the Big Bang is a spacelike past boundary, each observer sees only the part of the universe inside its past light cone. [§27.12](r2r:chapter_27#2712-共形图)
  - [ ] Where does the second law come from? {kind: question} ^q-arrow-of-time
    - Time-symmetric dynamics plus phase-space volumes give entropy increase both ways; the asymmetry must come from a low-entropy initial state. [§27.5](r2r:chapter_27#275-第二定律的导出)
  - [ ] Is the whole universe an isolated system? {kind: question} ^q-universe-isolated-system
    - Penrose rejects open-system escapes and the idea that expansion alone raises the entropy ceiling. [§27.6](r2r:chapter_27#276-整个宇宙可看作一个孤立系统吗)
  - [ ] Why was the Big Bang so special? {kind: question} ^q-special-big-bang
    - Thermal matter but uniform geometry made the initial state about one part in 10^(10^123) of phase space; gravity was not thermalized. [§27.13](r2r:chapter_27#2713-异乎寻常的特殊大爆炸)

- [ ] 28. Speculative theories of the early universe {kind: chapter} ^ch28
  - Critically examines spontaneous symmetry breaking, topological defects, inflation, the anthropic principle and the Hartle-Hawking proposal, and advances the Weyl curvature hypothesis for the special Big Bang. [Chapter 28](r2r:chapter_28)
  - [ ] Mexican hat potential {kind: physics} ^mexican-hat-potential
    - Energy landscape whose symmetric top is unstable; the state rolls to a randomly chosen point on the rim, breaking the symmetry. [§28.1](r2r:chapter_28#281-早期宇宙的自发对称破缺)
  - [ ] Phase transition {kind: physics} ^phase-transition
    - Abrupt change of equilibrium properties on cooling, e.g. a ferromagnet magnetizing below its Curie point; in QFT a change of vacuum. [§28.1](r2r:chapter_28#281-早期宇宙的自发对称破缺)
  - [ ] Cosmic topological defects {kind: physics} ^topological-defects
    - Defects such as domain walls, strings and monopoles that cannot be smoothed away when symmetry breaks independently in different regions. [§28.2](r2r:chapter_28#282-宇宙的拓扑缺陷)
  - [ ] Dirac and gauge monopoles {kind: physics} ^magnetic-monopoles
    - Isolated magnetic poles; Dirac showed one would force charge quantization, and grand unified theories predict non-Abelian gauge monopoles. [§28.2](r2r:chapter_28#282-宇宙的拓扑缺陷)
  - [ ] Cosmic strings {kind: physics} ^cosmic-strings
    - Line-like defects whose gravitational field is a 'cut and paste' wedge removed from Minkowski space, once proposed as seeds of galaxies. [§28.2](r2r:chapter_28#282-宇宙的拓扑缺陷)
  - [ ] Inflationary cosmology {kind: physics} ^inflationary-cosmology
    - Proposal that a brief exponential expansion very early on diluted monopoles and explains the universe's uniformity and flatness. [§28.4](r2r:chapter_28#284-暴胀宇宙学)
  - [ ] Horizon problem {kind: physics} ^horizon-problem
    - Distant regions with equal background temperature lie outside each other's particle horizons in the standard model, so cannot have thermalized. [§28.4](r2r:chapter_28#284-暴胀宇宙学)
  - [ ] Flatness and smoothness problems {kind: physics} ^flatness-problem
    - Why the universe is so nearly spatially flat and uniform; inflation claims to iron out initial irregularities. [§28.4](r2r:chapter_28#284-暴胀宇宙学)
  - [ ] De Sitter space {kind: math} ^de-sitter-space
    - Lorentzian 4-sphere in 5-dimensional Minkowski space, a vacuum solution with positive Lambda; its half gives the steady-state model. [§28.4](r2r:chapter_28#284-暴胀宇宙学)
  - [ ] Anti-de Sitter space {kind: math} ^anti-de-sitter-space
    - Constant-curvature spacetime with negative Lambda, containing closed timelike curves unless unwrapped to its universal cover. [§28.4](r2r:chapter_28#284-暴胀宇宙学)
  - [ ] Inflaton field and false vacuum {kind: physics} ^inflaton-field
    - Hypothetical scalar field whose potential's false-vacuum energy acts as a huge effective cosmological constant driving inflation. [§28.4](r2r:chapter_28#284-暴胀宇宙学)
  - [ ] Anthropic principle (weak and strong) {kind: physics} ^anthropic-principle
    - Observers can only find themselves where, or in universes whose constants, permit intelligent life; applied by Dicke, Carter and Hoyle. [§28.6](r2r:chapter_28#286-人存原理)
  - [ ] Ricci versus Weyl focusing {kind: physics} ^ricci-weyl-focusing
    - Ricci curvature from matter focuses light like a positive lens; Weyl curvature from free gravity acts as a pure astigmatic lens. [§28.8](r2r:chapter_28#288-外尔曲率假说)
  - [ ] Weyl curvature hypothesis {kind: physics} ^weyl-curvature-hypothesis
    - Penrose's proposal that Weyl curvature vanishes at initial singularities, unlike black-hole singularities, accounting for the low-entropy Big Bang. [§28.8](r2r:chapter_28#288-外尔曲率假说)
  - [ ] Naked singularities {kind: physics} ^naked-singularities
    - Timelike singularities visible from outside; cosmic censorship would leave only spacelike future and past singularities. [§28.8](r2r:chapter_28#288-外尔曲率假说)
  - [ ] Wick rotation and Euclideanization {kind: math} ^wick-rotation
    - Replacing t by i t turns Lorentzian metrics into Riemannian ones, used by Hawking to make gravitational path integrals tractable. [§28.9](r2r:chapter_28#289-哈特尔霍金的无界假说)
  - [ ] Hartle-Hawking no-boundary proposal {kind: physics} ^no-boundary-proposal
    - Quantum gravity proposal that a path integral over Riemannian geometries closes off the Big Bang smoothly, without a singular boundary. [§28.9](r2r:chapter_28#289-哈特尔霍金的无界假说)
  - [ ] Dark matter {kind: physics} ^dark-matter
    - Non-baryonic matter inferred from galactic rotation, far exceeding ordinary matter and bearing on the sign of spatial curvature K. [§28.10](r2r:chapter_28#2810-宇宙学参数观察的地位)
  - [ ] Dark energy and accelerating expansion {kind: physics} ^dark-energy
    - Supernova and microwave-background data from 1998 on indicate positive Lambda, now called dark energy, with K near zero. [§28.10](r2r:chapter_28#2810-宇宙学参数观察的地位)
  - [ ] Are the motivations for inflation valid? {kind: question} ^q-inflation-motivation
    - Penrose argues inflation cannot explain uniformity, since a generic initial state would not be smoothed and the second law forbids it. [§28.5](r2r:chapter_28#285-暴胀的动机有效吗)
  - [ ] Was early-universe symmetry breaking physically real? {kind: question} ^q-symmetry-breaking-reality
    - Causally separate regions would break electroweak symmetry independently, yet photons agree everywhere, challenging a literal broken-symmetry picture. [§28.3](r2r:chapter_28#283-早期宇宙的对称性破缺问题)
  - [ ] Can the anthropic principle explain the special Big Bang? {kind: question} ^q-anthropic-special-big-bang
    - No: life needs only a small smooth region, far cheaper in phase-space volume than the observed uniform universe. [§28.7](r2r:chapter_28#287-大爆炸的特殊性质人存是关键)
  - [ ] Why is the cosmological constant so small? {kind: question} ^q-cosmological-constant-problem
    - QFT vacuum-energy estimates exceed the observed Lambda by about 10^120, and the coincidence of Omega values remains puzzling. [§28.10](r2r:chapter_28#2810-宇宙学参数观察的地位)

- [ ] 29. The measurement paradox {kind: chapter} ^ch29
  - Penrose surveys the conventional and unconventional ontologies of quantum mechanics, introduces density matrices and the Bloch sphere, and argues that none resolves the Schrodinger's cat paradox without an objective R. [Chapter 29](r2r:chapter_29)
  - [ ] Copenhagen interpretation {kind: physics} ^copenhagen-interpretation
    - View (a): the state vector describes only the experimenter's knowledge, and R is an update of that knowledge, not a physical process. [Chapter 29](r2r:chapter_29)
  - [ ] Many-worlds interpretation (omnium) {kind: physics} ^many-worlds-interpretation
    - Everett's view (b): only U happens; all outcomes coexist in one vast superposition, the omnium, with observers split among them. [Chapter 29](r2r:chapter_29)
  - [ ] Consistent histories {kind: physics} ^consistent-histories
    - View (d): coarse-grained histories with inserted projector sets, selected so quantum probabilities obey classical probability rules. [§29.2](r2r:chapter_29#292-量子理论的非传统本体论)
  - [ ] De Broglie-Bohm pilot-wave theory {kind: physics} ^pilot-wave-theory
    - View (e): an actual configuration point in configuration space is guided by the wavefunction, giving two levels of reality. [§29.2](r2r:chapter_29#292-量子理论的非传统本体论)
  - [ ] Spectral decomposition into projectors {kind: math} ^spectral-decomposition
    - A Hermitian operator is a sum of its distinct eigenvalues times mutually orthogonal projectors summing to the identity. [§29.2](r2r:chapter_29#292-量子理论的非传统本体论)
  - [ ] Density matrix {kind: physics} ^density-matrix
    - Von Neumann's weighted sum of outer products of possible states; it alone yields all measurement probabilities, as trace of ED. [§29.3](r2r:chapter_29#293-密度矩阵)
  - [ ] Pure and mixed states {kind: physics} ^mixed-states
    - Density matrices of the form |psi><psi| are pure; others are mixed, and one mixed density matrix has many inequivalent decompositions. [§29.4](r2r:chapter_29#294-自旋-frac12-的密度矩阵布洛赫球)
  - [ ] Reduced density matrix of an entangled state {kind: physics} ^reduced-density-matrix
    - Summing over the unknown partner of an EPR pair (index contraction) gives a density matrix independent of the distant measurement choice. [§29.5](r2r:chapter_29#295-epr-状态的密度矩阵)
  - [ ] Environmental decoherence (FAPP) {kind: physics} ^environmental-decoherence
    - View (c): tracing out an inaccessible environment yields a diagonal density matrix; Penrose calls it a 'for all practical purposes' stopgap. [§29.6](r2r:chapter_29#296-环境退相关的-fapp-哲学)
  - [ ] Schrodinger's cat {kind: physics} ^schrodinger-cat
    - A beam-splitter photon triggers a cat-killing device, so U evolution yields a superposition of live and dead cats. [§29.7](r2r:chapter_29#297-哥本哈根本体论的薛定谔猫)
  - [ ] Schmidt decomposition {kind: math} ^schmidt-decomposition
    - An entangled state pairs a unique orthogonal basis on each side, but these preferred states need not be live and dead cat. [§29.8](r2r:chapter_29#298-其他传统本体论能够解决猫佯谬吗)
  - [ ] Is the quantum state real? {kind: question} ^q-reality-of-quantum-state
    - Views (a) to (f) differ on whether |psi>, a density matrix, or something else represents physical reality. [§29.2](r2r:chapter_29#292-量子理论的非传统本体论)
  - [ ] What reality does a density matrix describe? {kind: question} ^q-density-matrix-ontology
    - Since one density matrix admits many probability mixtures of states, it cannot fix which states are actually present. [§29.4](r2r:chapter_29#294-自旋-frac12-的密度矩阵布洛赫球)

- [ ] 30. Gravity's role in quantum state reduction {kind: chapter} ^ch30
  - Penrose argues from time asymmetry, black-hole thermodynamics and information loss, and the clash between superposition and general covariance that state reduction is an objective gravitational effect, testable by experiments like FELIX. [Chapter 30](r2r:chapter_30)
  - [ ] Anomalies {kind: physics} ^quantum-anomalies
    - Classical symmetries expressed by Poisson brackets that fail to survive quantization, leaving only a subgroup of the symmetry. [§30.2](r2r:chapter_30#302-来自宇宙学时间不对称的线索)
  - [ ] Time asymmetry of state reduction {kind: physics} ^time-asymmetry-of-r
    - A beam-splitter thought experiment shows the squared-modulus rule gives correct future probabilities but absurd retrodictions; R is time-asymmetric. [§30.3](r2r:chapter_30#303-量子态收缩的时间不对称性)
  - [ ] Hawking temperature {kind: physics} ^hawking-temperature
    - A black hole radiates thermally at a temperature proportional to surface gravity, found by QFT in curved space separating positive frequencies at scri. [§30.4](r2r:chapter_30#304-霍金的黑洞温度)
  - [ ] Unruh effect {kind: physics} ^unruh-effect
    - A uniformly accelerating observer sees the Minkowski vacuum as a thermal bath, because acceleration changes the positive-frequency notion. [§30.4](r2r:chapter_30#304-霍金的黑洞温度)
  - [ ] Rindler coordinates {kind: physics} ^rindler-coordinates
    - Hyperbolic-angle coordinates on Minkowski space whose time is the proper time of a uniformly accelerating observer, analogous to polar angle. [§30.5](r2r:chapter_30#305-源自复周期性的黑洞温度)
  - [ ] Gibbons-Perry complex-periodicity temperature {kind: physics} ^complex-periodicity-temperature
    - Complexifying the Schwarzschild or Kerr solution gives periodicity in imaginary time equal to the inverse Hawking temperature; elegant but not a physical derivation. [§30.5](r2r:chapter_30#305-源自复周期性的黑洞温度)
  - [ ] Eternal black hole and wormhole {kind: physics} ^eternal-black-hole
    - Maximally extended Schwarzschild spacetime: two exterior universes joined by a non-traversable wormhole, with black and white hole regions. [§30.6](r2r:chapter_30#306-基灵矢量能量流时间旅行)
  - [ ] Wormhole time travel {kind: physics} ^time-travel-wormholes
    - Holding a wormhole open needs negative energy density; travel between spacelike-separated events would permit closed timelike curves. [§30.6](r2r:chapter_30#306-基灵矢量能量流时间旅行)
  - [ ] Hawking radiation {kind: physics} ^hawking-radiation
    - Heuristically, virtual pairs at the horizon split: a negative-energy partner falls in where the Killing vector is spacelike, the other escapes. [§30.7](r2r:chapter_30#307-来自负能量途径的能量流)
  - [ ] Ergosphere and Penrose process {kind: physics} ^penrose-process
    - Outside a Kerr horizon the Killing vector turns spacelike, so particles can carry negative energy in, extracting rotational energy (quasar power). [§30.7](r2r:chapter_30#307-来自负能量途径的能量流)
  - [ ] Black-hole evaporation and Hawking explosion {kind: physics} ^hawking-evaporation
    - Once the ambient temperature drops below the hole's, it loses mass, heats up, and finally disappears in a small explosion. [§30.8](r2r:chapter_30#308-霍金爆炸)
  - [ ] Hawking's box and phase-space balance {kind: physics} ^hawking-box
    - A closed mirrored box shows phase-space volume lost at black-hole singularities must be balanced, Penrose suggests, by volume regained through R. [§30.9](r2r:chapter_30#309-更激进的观点)
  - [ ] Schrodinger lump {kind: physics} ^schrodinger-lump
    - A cat replaced by a massive lump superposed in two locations; each location is stationary, so standard QM deems the superposition stationary too. [§30.10](r2r:chapter_30#3010-薛定谔团块)
  - [ ] Gravitational self-energy of the difference E_G {kind: physics} ^gravitational-self-energy-difference
    - Superposing two spacetimes violates general covariance; the error, measured by the self-energy of the difference of mass distributions, is E_G. [§30.11](r2r:chapter_30#3011-与爱因斯坦原理的基本冲突)
  - [ ] Schrodinger-Newton equation {kind: physics} ^schrodinger-newton-equation
    - Schrodinger equation with a Newtonian potential sourced by the wavefunction's expected mass density; its stationary states are the preferred basis. [§30.12](r2r:chapter_30#3012-优先的薛定谔牛顿态)
  - [ ] Gravitational objective reduction {kind: physics} ^gravitational-or
    - A superposition of two stationary states spontaneously reduces to one in average time about hbar/E_G, without violating energy conservation. [§30.12](r2r:chapter_30#3012-优先的薛定谔牛顿态)
  - [ ] FELIX experiment {kind: physics} ^felix-experiment
    - A proposed interferometer putting a tiny mirror into superposed positions to test whether coherence is lost on the gravitational OR timescale. [§30.13](r2r:chapter_30#3013-felix-及其相关理论)
  - [ ] Is information lost in black holes? {kind: question} ^q-black-hole-information-loss
    - Loss, storage or return? Penrose sides with loss, arguing it violates unitarity and is balanced by objective R. [§30.8](r2r:chapter_30#308-霍金爆炸)
  - [ ] Is state reduction a gravitational effect? {kind: question} ^q-gravity-and-state-reduction
    - The conflict between quantum superposition and general covariance suggests R is objective and gravitational, a claim experiment could test. [§30.11](r2r:chapter_30#3011-与爱因斯坦原理的基本冲突)
  - [ ] What seeded early-universe irregularities? {kind: question} ^q-origin-of-early-universe-fluctuations
    - U evolution preserves exact FLRW symmetry, so 'quantum fluctuations' can break it only via some objective reduction, not FAPP horizon arguments. [§30.14](r2r:chapter_30#3014-早期宇宙涨落的起源)

- [ ] 31. Supersymmetry, supra-dimensionality, and strings {kind: chapter} ^ch31
  - Penrose explains supersymmetry, higher-dimensional spacetimes and the evolution of string theory into superstrings, M-theory, branes and holography, while critically questioning extra-dimensional stability, finiteness and string theory's physical status. [Chapter 31](r2r:chapter_31)
  - [ ] Running coupling constants {kind: physics} ^running-coupling-constants
    - Coupling strengths depend on interaction energy; observed constants are low-energy limits, and GUTs need them to meet at high energy. [§31.1](r2r:chapter_31#311-令人费解的参数)
  - [ ] Supersymmetry {kind: physics} ^supersymmetry
    - A symmetry pairing bosons with fermions via superpartners differing by spin 1/2, helping cancel QFT infinities; unsupported by observation. [§31.2](r2r:chapter_31#312-超对称)
  - [ ] Super-Lie algebras and supergroups {kind: math} ^super-lie-algebra
    - Lie algebras generalized with anticommutators among odd generators, whose parameters are Grassmann elements rather than real numbers. [§31.2](r2r:chapter_31#312-超对称)
  - [ ] Superalgebra and supermanifolds {kind: math} ^superalgebra
    - Adjoining anticommuting generators with epsilon squared zero to the reals; geometrically, a first-order neighbourhood of a manifold in a larger one. [§31.3](r2r:chapter_31#313-超对称代数和几何)
  - [ ] Supergravity {kind: physics} ^supergravity
    - General relativity made supersymmetric, adding a spin-3/2 gravitino; hoped renormalizable, with an 11-dimensional version later central to M-theory. [§31.3](r2r:chapter_31#313-超对称代数和几何)
  - [ ] Higher-dimensional spacetime and functional freedom {kind: physics} ^higher-dimensional-spacetime
    - Extra small dimensions as in Kaluza-Klein; without symmetry they bring far too much functional freedom compared with 4D physics. [§31.4](r2r:chapter_31#314-高维时空)
  - [ ] Original hadronic string theory {kind: physics} ^hadronic-string-theory
    - Veneziano's formula and Nambu's strings explained duality of hadron scattering diagrams and linear Regge trajectories via string history topology. [§31.5](r2r:chapter_31#315-原初的强子弦论)
  - [ ] Regge trajectories {kind: physics} ^regge-trajectories
    - Observed linear relations between spin and squared mass of hadron resonances, explained by strings whose tension grows with stretching. [§31.5](r2r:chapter_31#315-原初的强子弦论)
  - [ ] Superstring theory {kind: physics} ^superstrings
    - Anomaly cancellation forced 26 dimensions, reduced to 10 by supersymmetry (Green-Schwarz 1984); Planck-scale strings yield a spin-2 graviton. [§31.7](r2r:chapter_31#317-额外时空维的弦动机)
  - [ ] String worldsheet and Ricci-flatness {kind: physics} ^string-worldsheet
    - Strings sweep 2D worldsheets with an area Lagrangian; consistency demands Ricci-flat 10D background to lowest order in alpha prime. [§31.9](r2r:chapter_31#319-弦动力学)
  - [ ] Kaluza-Klein excitation modes {kind: physics} ^kaluza-klein-modes
    - Modes around a small compact dimension appear in 4D as Klein-Gordon particles with Planck-scale masses, hence supposedly unobservable. [§31.10](r2r:chapter_31#3110-为什么我们看不见额外的空间维)
  - [ ] Calabi-Yau spaces {kind: math} ^calabi-yau-spaces
    - Compact Ricci-flat Kahler 6-manifolds with covariantly constant spinors, required for the extra dimensions; thousands exist, ruining uniqueness. [§31.14](r2r:chapter_31#3114-神奇的卡拉比丘空间m-理论)
  - [ ] Mirror symmetry {kind: math} ^mirror-symmetry
    - A conjectured duality between Calabi-Yau spaces that correctly predicted counts of rational curves on a quintic threefold. [§31.14](r2r:chapter_31#3114-神奇的卡拉比丘空间m-理论)
  - [ ] String dualities (S, T, U) {kind: physics} ^string-dualities
    - Witten's 1995 second superstring revolution: the five superstring theories related by dualities exchanging strong and weak, small and large. [§31.14](r2r:chapter_31#3114-神奇的卡拉比丘空间m-理论)
  - [ ] M-theory {kind: physics} ^m-theory
    - A hypothetical 11-dimensional theory unifying the five string theories and 11D supergravity, containing p-branes of various dimensions. [§31.14](r2r:chapter_31#3114-神奇的卡拉比丘空间m-理论)
  - [ ] BPS states {kind: physics} ^bps-states
    - Supersymmetric extremal states (mass equals charge) protected from corrections, used to extrapolate weak-coupling string counts to black holes. [§31.15](r2r:chapter_31#3115-弦与黑洞熵)
  - [ ] String counting of black-hole entropy {kind: physics} ^string-black-hole-entropy
    - Strominger-Vafa count of BPS string states reproduces S = A/4 for extremal charged holes; Penrose doubts it explains horizon entropy. [§31.15](r2r:chapter_31#3115-弦与黑洞熵)
  - [ ] Holographic principle and AdS/CFT {kind: physics} ^holographic-principle
    - Maldacena's conjecture that string theory on AdS5 x S5 equals supersymmetric Yang-Mills on its conformal boundary, despite mismatched functional freedom. [§31.16](r2r:chapter_31#3116-全息原理)
  - [ ] D-branes and brane worlds {kind: physics} ^d-branes
    - Timelike subspaces on which open strings end; perhaps we live on a 3-brane, aimed at the hierarchy problem. [§31.17](r2r:chapter_31#3117-d-膜观点)
  - [ ] Why do nature's constants have their values? {kind: question} ^q-puzzling-parameters
    - The fine-structure constant, particle masses and the hierarchy problem (gravity's weakness) lack explanation, and renormalization cannot supply them. [§31.1](r2r:chapter_31#311-令人费解的参数)
  - [ ] Why don't we see the extra dimensions? {kind: question} ^q-why-unseen-extra-dimensions
    - Penrose doubts the quantum-stability argument and shows classically the extra dimensions are unstable, developing singularities. [§31.10](r2r:chapter_31#3110-为什么我们看不见额外的空间维)
  - [ ] Is string theory a finite quantum gravity? {kind: question} ^q-string-quantum-gravity
    - Finiteness is unproven beyond low orders and the genus sum likely diverges; the theory treats gravity only perturbatively on a background. [§31.13](r2r:chapter_31#3113-弦量子场论是有限的吗)
  - [ ] What is the physical status of string theory? {kind: question} ^q-status-of-string-theory
    - Remarkable mathematical coincidences such as mirror symmetry impress, but give no clear reason to believe the theory describes nature. [§31.18](r2r:chapter_31#3118-弦论的物理学地位)

- [ ] 32. Einstein's narrower path; loop variables {kind: chapter} ^ch32
  - Penrose surveys canonical quantum gravity through Ashtekar's chiral variables, loop variables and spin networks, weighing the successes and open difficulties of loop quantum gravity. [Chapter 32](r2r:chapter_32)
  - [ ] Canonical quantum gravity {kind: physics} ^canonical-quantum-gravity
    - Quantizing general relativity from its Hamiltonian form on a spacelike 3-surface, hampered by general covariance and non-polynomial Hamiltonians. [§32.1](r2r:chapter_32#321-正则量子引力)
  - [ ] Constraint equations of general relativity {kind: physics} ^constraint-equations
    - Equations acting within the initial 3-surface that data must satisfy for consistent evolution; Dirac developed quantization methods to handle them. [§32.1](r2r:chapter_32#321-正则量子引力)
  - [ ] Spin-2 graviton and its helicities {kind: physics} ^graviton-helicity
    - Gravitational waves return to themselves under a rotation by pi, so gravitons have spin 2 with helicities plus and minus 2. [§32.2](r2r:chapter_32#322-阿什台卡变量的手征输入)
  - [ ] Self-dual and anti-self-dual Weyl curvature {kind: math} ^self-dual-weyl-curvature
    - Splitting the complex Weyl tensor via its dual into parts describing right- and left-handed gravitons, as for the Maxwell field. [§32.2](r2r:chapter_32#322-阿什台卡变量的手征输入)
  - [ ] Ashtekar variables {kind: physics} ^ashtekar-variables
    - Chiral canonical variables (unprimed spin connection and 3-metric densities) that render the gravitational constraints polynomial. [§32.3](r2r:chapter_32#323-阿什台卡变量的形式)
  - [ ] Barbero-Immirzi parameter {kind: physics} ^barbero-immirzi-parameter
    - Real parameter eta replacing i in the connection, turning the noncompact group SL(2,C) into compact SU(2) at the cost of chirality. [§32.3](r2r:chapter_32#323-阿什台卡变量的形式)
  - [ ] Wilson loops (holonomy traces) {kind: math} ^wilson-loops
    - The trace of the spin-space transformation obtained by parallel transport around a closed loop, a basis-independent quantity of the connection. [§32.4](r2r:chapter_32#324-圈变量)
  - [ ] Loop variables and loop states {kind: physics} ^loop-variables
    - Quantum-gravity basis states concentrated on loops in the 3-surface, with delta-function area and only topological meaning. [§32.4](r2r:chapter_32#324-圈变量)
  - [ ] Quantized area spectrum {kind: physics} ^area-quantization
    - Each crossing of a probe surface by a loop of spin j contributes area 8 pi G eta hbar sqrt(j(j+1)). [§32.4](r2r:chapter_32#324-圈变量)
  - [ ] Knots and links {kind: math} ^knots-and-links
    - Topology of closed loops in 3-space: trefoil, Whitehead link, Borromean rings, and polynomial invariants like Alexander and Jones. [§32.5](r2r:chapter_32#325-结与链的数学)
  - [ ] Jones polynomial and knot invariants {kind: math} ^jones-polynomial
    - Physics-inspired knot invariants (Jones, HOMFLY, Kauffman) arising from a diagrammatic algebra that distinguishes over- and under-crossings. [§32.5](r2r:chapter_32#325-结与链的数学)
  - [ ] q-deformed structures (quantum groups) {kind: math} ^q-deformation
    - Kauffman algebra with q = A^2 deforms the binor calculus; misleadingly called 'quantum groups' though unrelated to quantum theory. [§32.5](r2r:chapter_32#325-结与链的数学)
  - [ ] Topological quantum field theory {kind: physics} ^topological-quantum-field-theory
    - Witten's theories with no local field equations, only global structure and source glitches, like 2+1-dimensional general relativity. [§32.5](r2r:chapter_32#325-结与链的数学)
  - [ ] Spin networks {kind: physics} ^spin-networks
    - Penrose's combinatorial networks of total-spin units with trivalent vertices giving rational probabilities, from which 3-space directions emerge; reused in loop gravity. [§32.6](r2r:chapter_32#326-自旋网络)
  - [ ] Loop-gravity black-hole entropy {kind: physics} ^black-hole-entropy-loop
    - Counting spin-network states on a horizon reproduces Bekenstein-Hawking entropy when eta = log 2/(pi sqrt 3). [§32.6](r2r:chapter_32#326-自旋网络)
  - [ ] Spin foams {kind: physics} ^spin-foams
    - Higher-dimensional spin networks with spin-labelled 2-surfaces, viewed as the time evolution of spin networks. [§32.7](r2r:chapter_32#327-圈量子引力的地位)
  - [ ] Is gravity fundamentally left-right asymmetric? {kind: question} ^q-chirality-of-gravity
    - Ashtekar's chiral variables treat graviton helicities differently; is this a mathematical artifact or a hint of deep natural chirality? [§32.2](r2r:chapter_32#322-阿什台卡变量的手征输入)
  - [ ] Hamiltonian constraint and dynamics in loop gravity {kind: question} ^q-hamiltonian-constraint
    - Loop gravity solves the spatial constraints but lacks an unambiguous treatment of evolution off the 3-surface. [§32.6](r2r:chapter_32#326-自旋网络)
  - [ ] Problem of time in quantum gravity {kind: question} ^q-problem-of-time
    - General covariance makes time evolution hard to identify, and superposed spin networks have no correspondence between their locations. [§32.7](r2r:chapter_32#327-圈量子引力的地位)

- [ ] 33. More radical perspectives; twistor theory {kind: chapter} ^ch33
  - After surveying discrete approaches to spacetime, Penrose develops twistor theory, in which light rays and holomorphic structures are primary and spacetime points are derived. [Chapter 33](r2r:chapter_33)
  - [ ] Causal sets {kind: physics} ^causal-sets
    - Sorkin's proposal that spacetime is a discrete, possibly finite set of points whose causal relations are the basic notion. [§33.1](r2r:chapter_33#331-几何上具有离散元素的理论)
  - [ ] Regge calculus {kind: physics} ^regge-calculus
    - Spacetime built from flat 4-simplices with curvature concentrated as delta functions on 2-dimensional 'bones'. [§33.1](r2r:chapter_33#331-几何上具有离散元素的理论)
  - [ ] Category theory and topos theory {kind: math} ^category-theory
    - Very general abstract algebraic frameworks (with n-categories and intuitionistic topos logic) proposed as possible foundations for physics. [§33.1](r2r:chapter_33#331-几何上具有离散元素的理论)
  - [ ] Noncommutative geometry {kind: math} ^noncommutative-geometry
    - Connes's reconstruction of 'spaces' from noncommutative algebras, generalizing how a manifold is recovered from its function algebra. [§33.1](r2r:chapter_33#331-几何上具有离散元素的理论)
  - [ ] Twistors as light rays {kind: math} ^twistor-correspondence
    - Light rays become points of PN, while spacetime events become Riemann spheres (celestial spheres), making light rays more basic than points. [§33.2](r2r:chapter_33#332-作为光线的扭量)
  - [ ] Conformal group of spacetime {kind: math} ^conformal-group
    - The group O(2,4) preserving light cones, under which massless field equations are invariant; larger than the Poincare group. [§33.3](r2r:chapter_33#333-共形群紧化闵可夫斯基空间)
  - [ ] Compactified Minkowski space {kind: math} ^compactified-minkowski-space
    - M# obtained by adjoining a light cone at infinity, realized as rays of the null cone in E(2,4), with topology S1 x S3. [§33.3](r2r:chapter_33#333-共形群紧化闵可夫斯基空间)
  - [ ] Twistors and twistor space {kind: math} ^twistors
    - Elements of a 4-complex-dimensional space T with Hermitian form of signature (++--); reduced spinors of O(2,4), locally SU(2,2). [§33.5](r2r:chapter_33#335-基本扭量几何及其坐标)
  - [ ] Projective twistor space and incidence relation {kind: math} ^projective-twistor-space
    - PT = CP3 split by the null region PN into PT+ and PT-; the incidence relation omega = i r pi links twistors and events. [§33.5](r2r:chapter_33#335-基本扭量几何及其坐标)
  - [ ] Robinson congruence {kind: math} ^robinson-congruence
    - Twisting family of light rays representing a non-null twistor, picturing a massless spinning particle's nonlocal angular momentum. [§33.6](r2r:chapter_33#336-作为无质量自旋粒子的扭量的几何)
  - [ ] Alpha-planes and beta-planes {kind: math} ^alpha-planes
    - Self-dual complex 2-planes in complexified Minkowski space representing twistors; anti-self-dual beta-planes represent dual twistors. [§33.6](r2r:chapter_33#336-作为无质量自旋粒子的扭量的几何)
  - [ ] Twistor quantum theory {kind: physics} ^twistor-quantization
    - Z and Z-bar become canonically conjugate operators, so twistor wavefunctions are holomorphic; helicity is the Euler homogeneity operator. [§33.7](r2r:chapter_33#337-扭量量子论)
  - [ ] Massless free-field equations {kind: physics} ^massless-field-equations
    - Two-spinor equations for fields of any spin, generalizing the Weyl neutrino, Maxwell and linearized Weyl-curvature equations. [§33.8](r2r:chapter_33#338-无质量场的扭量描述)
  - [ ] Twistor contour integrals (Penrose transform) {kind: math} ^twistor-contour-integral
    - Contour integrals of homogeneous twistor functions automatically give solutions of massless field equations; positive frequency means extending into PT+. [§33.8](r2r:chapter_33#338-无质量场的扭量描述)
  - [ ] Sheaf cohomology {kind: math} ^sheaf-cohomology
    - First cohomology via Cech cocycles on overlapping patches, essentially nonlocal; twistor functions are holomorphic cohomology elements, illustrated by the impossible tribar. [§33.9](r2r:chapter_33#339-扭量层上同调)
  - [ ] Twistor positive/negative frequency splitting {kind: physics} ^twistor-frequency-splitting
    - Cohomology on PT+ and PT- gives positive- and negative-frequency fields, analogous to splitting functions on the Riemann sphere's equator. [§33.10](r2r:chapter_33#3310-扭量与正负频率剖分)
  - [ ] Nonlinear graviton construction {kind: physics} ^nonlinear-graviton
    - Deforming twistor space yields curved complex anti-self-dual vacuum spacetimes, with points as holomorphic curves via Kodaira's theorems. [§33.11](r2r:chapter_33#3311-非线性引力子)
  - [ ] Ward construction of anti-self-dual gauge fields {kind: physics} ^ward-construction
    - Twistor description of general anti-self-dual Yang-Mills fields via holomorphic bundles, analogous to the nonlinear graviton. [§33.12](r2r:chapter_33#3312-扭量与广义相对论)
  - [ ] Is spacetime fundamentally discrete? {kind: question} ^q-discrete-spacetime
    - Should the real-number continuum be replaced by finite fields, lattices, causal sets or other combinatorial structures? [§33.1](r2r:chapter_33#331-几何上具有离散元素的理论)
  - [ ] The googly problem {kind: question} ^q-googly-problem
    - How to obtain the right-handed nonlinear graviton from twistors rather than dual twistors, needed for full general relativity. [§33.12](r2r:chapter_33#3312-扭量与广义相对论)
  - [ ] Does cohomological nonlocality explain quantum nonlocality? {kind: question} ^q-twistor-nonlocality-epr
    - Penrose suspects the nonlocal nature of twistor cohomology is hinting at the mysterious nonlocality of EPR entanglement. [§33.9](r2r:chapter_33#339-扭量层上同调)
  - [ ] Can twistor theory make physical predictions? {kind: question} ^q-twistor-predictions
    - Twistor theory lacks clear predictions; its holomorphic philosophy suggests negative spatial curvature K<0 and perhaps Lambda=0. [§33.14](r2r:chapter_33#3314-扭量理论的未来)

- [ ] 34. Where lies the road to reality? {kind: chapter} ^ch34
  - Penrose assesses twentieth-century physics, the influence of fashion and mathematical beauty, the testability of theories, the nature of reality and the place of mind in physics. [Chapter 34](r2r:chapter_34)
  - [ ] Fullerene double-slit interference {kind: physics} ^fullerene-interference
    - Zeilinger's group showed quantum interference of C60 and C70 molecules, still far below the mass scale where gravitational OR would act. [§34.1](r2r:chapter_34#341-20-世纪物理学的伟大理论及其超越)
  - [ ] Popper's falsifiability criterion {kind: physics} ^popper-falsifiability
    - A theory is scientific only if experiment could refute it; Penrose finds the criterion too harsh, testing it on supersymmetry and inflation. [§34.4](r2r:chapter_34#344-错误理论能被实验驳倒吗)
  - [ ] Quintessence {kind: physics} ^quintessence
    - A new dynamical field with negative pressure supplying 'dark energy' as an effective cosmological constant, cited as fashion-driven cosmology. [§34.4](r2r:chapter_34#344-错误理论能被实验驳倒吗)
  - [ ] Orchestrated objective reduction (microtubules) {kind: physics} ^orch-or
    - Hameroff-Penrose proposal that large-scale quantum coherence in neuronal microtubules ends in orchestrated gravitational OR events underlying conscious acts. [§34.7](r2r:chapter_34#347-心智在物理理论中的作用)
  - [ ] Binding problem and Duggins's Bell test {kind: physics} ^binding-problem-bell-test
    - Duggins proposed seeking Bell-inequality violations in how separate brain areas bind perceptions, as evidence of quantum effects in consciousness. [§34.7](r2r:chapter_34#347-心智在物理理论中的作用)
  - [ ] Can a wrong theory be experimentally refuted? {kind: question} ^q-can-wrong-theories-be-refuted
    - Fashionable theories like supersymmetry, string theory and inflation can adjust their parameters to evade experimental refutation. [§34.4](r2r:chapter_34#344-错误理论能被实验驳倒吗)
  - [ ] What role does fashion play in physics? {kind: question} ^q-fashion-in-physics
    - Without experimental guidance, bandwagon effects steer research toward popular approaches such as string theory and four-spinor notation. [§34.3](r2r:chapter_34#343-物理理论中时尚的作用)
  - [ ] What is reality? {kind: question} ^q-what-is-reality
    - Physics describes reality through mathematical models; Penrose asks whether reality is ultimately mathematical and revisits the three worlds. [§34.6](r2r:chapter_34#346-什么是实在)
  - [ ] What role does mind play in physical theory? {kind: question} ^q-mind-in-physics
    - From the anthropic principle to interpretations of R, Penrose argues real OR governs consciousness rather than consciousness governing R. [§34.7](r2r:chapter_34#347-心智在物理理论中的作用)
  - [ ] Are beauty and 'miracles' reliable guides to physics? {kind: question} ^q-mathematical-beauty-as-guide
    - Mathematical beauty and surprising coincidences have guided progress yet often misled, as with quaternions, supergravity and string miracles. [§34.9](r2r:chapter_34#349-美和奇迹)

